        content: `# Wound Healing: Core Principles

Wound healing is a complex, dynamic process involving cellular, molecular, and biochemical mechanisms that restore tissue integrity after injury.

## Definition
Wound healing is the body's natural process of regenerating dermal and epidermal tissue following trauma, surgery, or disease.

## Types of Wound Healing

### Primary Intention (Prima Intentio)
| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Wound Type | Clean surgical incision, minimal tissue loss |
| Closure | Edges approximated with sutures, staples, or adhesives |
| Healing Time | 7-14 days |
| Scar Formation | Minimal, linear scar |
| Examples | Surgical incisions, lacerations with primary closure |

### Secondary Intention (Secunda Intentio)
| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Wound Type | Tissue loss, contaminated wounds, abscess cavities |
| Closure | Left open to heal by granulation and contraction |
| Healing Time | Weeks to months depending on size |
| Scar Formation | Significant scarring, contraction |
| Examples | Pressure ulcers, diabetic ulcers, open traumatic wounds |

### Tertiary Intention (Delayed Primary Closure)
| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Wound Type | Contaminated wounds with initial high bacterial load |
| Closure | Left open initially, closed surgically after 4-7 days |
| Healing Time | Intermediate |
| Examples | Contaminated traumatic wounds, abscess after drainage |

## Molecular Mediators of Wound Healing

### Key Growth Factors
| Growth Factor | Source | Primary Function |
|---------------|--------|------------------|
| PDGF | Platelets, macrophages | Chemotaxis, fibroblast proliferation |
| TGF-β | Platelets, macrophages, lymphocytes | Collagen synthesis, ECM deposition |
| EGF | Platelets, macrophages | Epithelial cell proliferation |
| VEGF | Macrophages, keratinocytes | Angiogenesis |
| FGF | Macrophages, fibroblasts | Angiogenesis, fibroblast proliferation |
| KGF (FGF-7) | Fibroblasts | Keratinocyte proliferation |
| IGF-1 | Macrophages, fibroblasts | Cell proliferation, collagen synthesis |

### Cytokines and Interleukins
| Cytokine | Role in Wound Healing |
|----------|----------------------|
| IL-1 | Inflammatory response, fibroblast activation |
| IL-6 | Acute phase response, angiogenesis |
| IL-8 | Neutrophil chemotaxis |
| TNF-α | Inflammation, macrophage activation |
| IL-10 | Anti-inflammatory, resolution of inflammation |

## Cellular Components

### Key Cell Types and Functions
| Cell Type | Peak Activity | Primary Function |
|-----------|---------------|------------------|
| Platelets | Immediate | Hemostasis, growth factor release |
| Neutrophils | 24-48 hours | Bacterial killing, debris removal |
| Macrophages | 48-72 hours | Phagocytosis, growth factor secretion, orchestration |
| Lymphocytes | Day 5-7 | Immune regulation, cytokine production |
| Fibroblasts | Day 3-14 | Collagen synthesis, ECM production |
| Myofibroblasts | Day 7-21 | Wound contraction |
| Keratinocytes | Day 1-14 | Re-epithelialization |
| Endothelial cells | Day 3-7 | Angiogenesis |

## Extracellular Matrix (ECM)

### Components and Functions
| Component | Function |
|-----------|----------|
| Fibronectin | Cell migration scaffold, provisional matrix |
| Collagen Type III | Early matrix, immature scar |
| Collagen Type I | Mature scar, tensile strength |
| Hyaluronic acid | Hydration, cell migration |
| Proteoglycans | Growth factor binding, tissue hydration |
| Elastin | Tissue elasticity (minimal in scar) |`,
