        content: `# The Four Phases of Wound Healing

## 1. Hemostasis Phase (Immediate - Minutes to Hours)

### Vascular Response
| Event | Timing | Mechanism |
|-------|--------|-----------|
| Vasoconstriction | 0-10 min | Endothelin, thromboxane A2, reflex |
| Platelet adhesion | 1-3 min | von Willebrand factor, collagen exposure |
| Platelet aggregation | 3-5 min | ADP, thromboxane A2 release |
| Coagulation cascade | 5-10 min | Intrinsic and extrinsic pathways |
| Fibrin clot formation | 10-30 min | Thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin |

### Coagulation Cascade
**Intrinsic Pathway**: Contact activation (Factor XII) → XI → IX → X → Common pathway
**Extrinsic Pathway**: Tissue factor + Factor VII → X → Common pathway
**Common Pathway**: Factor X → Prothrombin → Thrombin → Fibrinogen → Fibrin

### Platelet Functions
- Form hemostatic plug (primary hemostasis)
- Release growth factors: PDGF, TGF-β, EGF, VEGF
- Recruit inflammatory cells
- Provide scaffold for provisional matrix

## 2. Inflammatory Phase (0-7 days)

### Early Inflammatory Phase (0-48 hours)
| Cell Type | Peak Time | Function |
|-----------|-----------|----------|
| Neutrophils | 24-48 hrs | First responders, bacterial killing, debris removal |
| Mast cells | Hours | Histamine release, vasodilation |

**Neutrophil Functions:**
- Phagocytosis of bacteria and debris
- Release of proteases (elastase, collagenase)
- Reactive oxygen species production
- Essential for infection control, but NOT required for healing

### Late Inflammatory Phase (48-72 hours onward)
| Cell Type | Peak Time | Function |
|-----------|-----------|----------|
| Macrophages | 48-96 hrs | Master orchestrators of wound healing |
| Lymphocytes | 5-7 days | Immune modulation, cytokine production |

**Macrophage Phenotypes:**
| Phenotype | Markers | Function |
|-----------|---------|----------|
| M1 (Pro-inflammatory) | CD86, iNOS | Phagocytosis, bacterial killing, inflammation |
| M2 (Anti-inflammatory) | CD206, Arginase | Tissue repair, growth factor secretion, resolution |

**Critical Role of Macrophages:**
- Transition from M1 to M2 essential for healing progression
- Secrete growth factors (PDGF, TGF-β, VEGF, FGF)
- Clear apoptotic neutrophils
- Activate fibroblasts
- Macrophage dysfunction → chronic wound

## 3. Proliferative Phase (3 days - 3 weeks)

### Key Processes
| Process | Timing | Key Events |
|---------|--------|------------|
| Angiogenesis | Days 3-7 | New blood vessel formation from existing vessels |
| Fibroplasia | Days 4-14 | Fibroblast migration, proliferation, ECM synthesis |
| Granulation | Days 4-21 | Capillary loops + fibroblasts + ECM formation |
| Re-epithelialization | Days 1-14 | Keratinocyte migration and proliferation |
| Wound contraction | Days 7-21 | Myofibroblast-mediated wound size reduction |

### Angiogenesis
**Stimuli:** Hypoxia, VEGF, FGF, PDGF
**Process:**
1. Endothelial cell activation
2. Basement membrane degradation (MMPs)
3. Endothelial cell migration and proliferation
4. Tube formation and vessel maturation
5. Blood flow establishment

### Granulation Tissue Composition
- Capillary loops (explains red color)
- Fibroblasts
- Macrophages
- Collagen (primarily Type III)
- Proteoglycans and glycosaminoglycans
- Fibronectin matrix

### Re-epithelialization
**Keratinocyte Migration:**
- Begins within hours from wound edge and adnexal structures
- Contact inhibition guides directional migration
- Leapfrog migration pattern
- Integrin expression changes allow migration on provisional matrix
- Complete when epithelial edges meet

### Collagen Synthesis
| Collagen Type | Timing | Characteristics |
|---------------|--------|-----------------|
| Type III | Early (days-weeks) | Fine fibrils, less tensile strength |
| Type I | Late (weeks-months) | Thick fibrils, provides strength |
| Ratio Type III:I | Wound vs. skin | 30:70 vs. 20:80 |

## 4. Remodeling/Maturation Phase (3 weeks - 2+ years)

### Key Events
| Process | Timeline | Description |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Collagen remodeling | 3 wks - 1 yr | Type III → Type I replacement |
| Cross-linking | Ongoing | Lysyl oxidase creates collagen cross-links |
| Scar maturation | 6-18 months | Red → pink → pale/white |
| Contraction | Ongoing | Continued wound contraction |
| MMP/TIMP balance | Ongoing | Matrix turnover regulation |

### Tensile Strength Recovery
| Time | % of Original Strength |
|------|----------------------|
| 1 week | 3% |
| 3 weeks | 20% |
| 3 months | 50% |
| 1 year | 70-80% |
| Maximum | 80% (never returns to 100%) |

### Matrix Metalloproteinases (MMPs)
| MMP | Substrate | Role |
|-----|-----------|------|
| MMP-1 (Collagenase) | Collagen I, II, III | Collagen breakdown |
| MMP-2 (Gelatinase A) | Gelatin, collagen IV | Basement membrane remodeling |
| MMP-9 (Gelatinase B) | Gelatin, collagen IV | Cell migration, angiogenesis |
| MMP-3 (Stromelysin) | Proteoglycans, fibronectin | ECM remodeling |

### Tissue Inhibitors of Metalloproteinases (TIMPs)
- TIMP-1, TIMP-2, TIMP-3, TIMP-4
- Balance with MMPs determines net matrix turnover
- Excess MMPs → chronic wound
- Excess TIMPs → excessive scarring`,
