        title: 'TIME Framework for Wound Bed Preparation',
        content: `# TIME Framework for Wound Bed Preparation

A systematic, evidence-based approach to wound bed preparation addressing barriers to healing developed by the International Wound Bed Preparation Advisory Board.

## T - Tissue Management

### Types of Non-Viable Tissue
| Type | Appearance | Characteristics |
|------|------------|-----------------|
| Necrotic/Eschar | Black, dry, leathery | Desiccated dead tissue, hard |
| Slough | Yellow/tan, soft, stringy | Devitalized tissue mixed with fibrin |
| Fibrin | Yellow, adherent | Protein matrix covering wound bed |
| Biofilm | Slimy, shiny coating | Bacterial communities in matrix |

### Debridement Methods

#### Sharp/Surgical Debridement
| Aspect | Details |
|--------|---------|
| Indications | Thick eschar, extensive necrosis, urgent wounds |
| Advantages | Fastest, most effective, removes biofilm |
| Disadvantages | Requires skill, may cause bleeding, pain |
| Contraindications | Ischemic limbs without revascularization, pyoderma gangrenosum |

#### Enzymatic Debridement
| Agent | Mechanism | Use |
|-------|-----------|-----|
| Collagenase (Santyl) | Digests collagen in necrotic tissue | Slough and necrotic tissue |
| Papain-urea | Proteolytic enzyme + protein denaturant | Discontinued in US |

#### Autolytic Debridement
| Aspect | Details |
|--------|---------|
| Mechanism | Body's own enzymes liquefy necrotic tissue |
| Method | Occlusive/moisture-retentive dressings |
| Advantages | Selective, painless, no special training |
| Disadvantages | Slow, contraindicated in infection |

#### Biological Debridement (Maggot Therapy)
| Aspect | Details |
|--------|---------|
| Organism | Lucilia sericata (green bottle fly larvae) |
| Mechanism | Secrete proteolytic enzymes, ingest necrotic tissue |
| Advantages | Selective, promotes granulation, antimicrobial |
| Use | Complex wounds, multidrug-resistant organisms |

#### Mechanical Debridement
| Method | Technique | Notes |
|--------|-----------|-------|
| Wet-to-dry dressings | Gauze dries and removes tissue | Non-selective, painful, outdated |
| Hydrotherapy | Pulsatile lavage, whirlpool | Reduces bacterial load |
| Ultrasonic debridement | Low-frequency ultrasound | Selective, promotes healing |

## I - Infection and Inflammation Control

### Wound Infection Continuum
| Stage | Bacterial Load | Signs | Management |
|-------|---------------|-------|------------|
| Contamination | Present, not multiplying | None | No treatment needed |
| Colonization | Multiplying, no host response | None | Monitoring |
| Critical colonization | Delayed healing | Subtle changes, stalled healing | Topical antimicrobials |
| Local infection | >10⁵/gram | Classic signs: erythema, warmth, purulence | Debridement + antimicrobials |
| Spreading infection | Invasion of surrounding tissue | Cellulitis, lymphangitis | Systemic antibiotics |
| Systemic infection | Sepsis | Fever, tachycardia, organ dysfunction | IV antibiotics, critical care |

### Signs of Wound Infection
**Classic Signs (NERDS - Superficial):**
- **N**on-healing despite optimal care
- **E**xudate increased
- **R**ed and bleeding granulation tissue (friable)
- **D**ebris (yellow or black)
- **S**mell (malodorous)

**Deep Infection Signs (STONEES):**
- **S**ize increasing
- **T**emperature elevated (surrounding skin)
- **O**s (bone) - probe to bone (osteomyelitis)
- **N**ew satellite areas of breakdown
- **E**xudate increased
- **E**rythema/Edema
- **S**mell

### Biofilm Management
| Aspect | Details |
|--------|---------|
| Definition | Bacterial communities encased in protective matrix |
| Prevalence | Present in 60-90% of chronic wounds |
| Detection | Clinical suspicion - no reliable bedside test |
| Management | Sharp debridement + topical antimicrobials |
| Prevention | Regular debridement disrupts reformation |

### Topical Antimicrobial Agents
| Agent | Spectrum | Advantages | Disadvantages |
|-------|----------|------------|---------------|
| Silver | Broad | Multiple formulations, low resistance | Cytotoxic at high concentrations |
| Iodine (cadexomer) | Broad | Absorbs exudate, sustained release | Thyroid concerns (large wounds) |
| PHMB | Broad | Low cytotoxicity, biofilm activity | Cost |
| Honey (medical grade) | Broad | Natural, promotes healing | Allergies, messy |
| Chlorhexidine | Broad | Available | Cytotoxic to fibroblasts |

## M - Moisture Balance

### Wound Moisture Continuum
| State | Characteristics | Consequences | Management |
|-------|-----------------|--------------|------------|
| Desiccated | Dry wound bed | Cell death, impaired migration | Hydrating dressings |
| Balanced | Moist, not wet | Optimal healing | Maintain with appropriate dressings |
| Excessive | Macerated periwound | Tissue breakdown, infection risk | Absorbent dressings, skin protectants |

### Dressing Selection by Exudate Level
| Exudate Level | Dressing Types | Examples |
|---------------|----------------|----------|
| None/Minimal | Hydrocolloids, films, hydrogels | Duoderm, Tegaderm, Intrasite |
| Low-Moderate | Foams, hydrofibers | Mepilex, Aquacel |
| Moderate-Heavy | Alginates, superabsorbents | Kaltostat, Drawtex |
| Heavy | Superabsorbents, NPWT | Vimex, wound VAC |

### Periwound Skin Protection
- Barrier films (Cavilon, Skin Prep)
- Zinc oxide paste
- Proper dressing size and adherence
- Address underlying cause of excess exudate

## E - Edge Effect/Epithelial Advancement

### Assessing Epithelial Advancement
| Finding | Interpretation | Action |
|---------|---------------|--------|
| Active migration | Pink epithelial edge advancing | Continue current therapy |
| Stalled edge | No advancement in 2-4 weeks | Reassess wound, consider advanced therapy |
| Rolled/epibole edges | Epithelium rolled under | May need debridement of edges |
| Undermined edges | Cavity under wound edge | Pack wound, address etiology |
| Hyperkeratotic edges | Thickened, callused | Debride callus, redistribute pressure |

### Causes of Non-Advancing Wound Edges
| Cause | Example | Intervention |
|-------|---------|--------------|
| Unaddressed etiology | Continued pressure, venous insufficiency | Offloading, compression |
| Biofilm | Bacterial communities | Debridement, antimicrobials |
| Senescent cells | Non-proliferating keratinocytes at edge | Edge debridement |
| Malnutrition | Protein-calorie deficiency | Nutritional optimization |
| Malignancy | Marjolin ulcer | Biopsy, oncologic care |
| Vasculitis | Autoimmune | Treat underlying condition |

### When to Consider Advanced Therapies
- No edge advancement after 2-4 weeks of optimal care
- <40% wound area reduction at 4 weeks predicts non-healing
- Consider: NPWT, skin substitutes, growth factors, HBOT, surgery`,
