        content: `# Burn Classification and Assessment

## Burn Depth Classification

### Superficial (First-Degree)
| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Layers involved | Epidermis only |
| Appearance | Red, dry, no blisters, blanches with pressure |
| Sensation | Painful to touch |
| Capillary refill | Brisk |
| Healing time | 3-7 days |
| Scarring | None |
| Treatment | Moisturizers, NSAIDs, no dressing required |
| Example | Sunburn, minor scald |

### Superficial Partial-Thickness (Second-Degree Superficial)
| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Layers involved | Epidermis + papillary (superficial) dermis |
| Appearance | Red, moist, intact or ruptured blisters, blanches briskly |
| Sensation | Very painful, hypersensitive |
| Capillary refill | Brisk (intact dermal plexus) |
| Healing time | 7-14 days |
| Scarring | Minimal if no infection |
| Treatment | Topical antimicrobials, non-adherent dressings |
| Example | Brief scald, flash burn |

### Deep Partial-Thickness (Second-Degree Deep)
| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Layers involved | Epidermis + extends into reticular (deep) dermis |
| Appearance | Pale pink to white, mottled, may have ruptured blisters |
| Sensation | Reduced pain sensation (deep nerve damage) |
| Capillary refill | Sluggish or absent |
| Healing time | 21-35 days if not grafted |
| Scarring | Hypertrophic scarring likely |
| Treatment | Often requires excision and grafting |
| Example | Prolonged contact with hot liquid, grease burns |

### Full-Thickness (Third-Degree)
| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Layers involved | Complete epidermis and dermis destruction |
| Appearance | White, waxy, brown, leathery, charred; no blanching |
| Sensation | Insensate (nerve endings destroyed) |
| Capillary refill | Absent |
| Healing time | Cannot heal spontaneously (no epithelial appendages) |
| Scarring | Dense scar from wound edges only |
| Treatment | Excision and skin grafting required |
| Example | Flame burns, prolonged contact, high-voltage electrical |

### Fourth-Degree
| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Layers involved | Extends through subcutaneous tissue to fascia, muscle, tendon, bone |
| Appearance | Charred, exposed deep structures |
| Sensation | Insensate |
| Treatment | Excision, possible amputation, flap coverage |
| Example | High-voltage electrical, prolonged flame exposure |

## Burn Depth Assessment Methods

### Clinical Assessment
| Method | Technique | Limitations |
|--------|-----------|-------------|
| Visual inspection | Color, moisture, texture | Subjective, evolving burn |
| Capillary refill | Apply pressure, assess blanching | Edema may obscure |
| Pain sensation | Pinprick testing | Area-dependent |
| Hair pull test | Easily epilated = deep | Not always reliable |

### Adjunctive Technologies
| Technology | Principle | Use |
|------------|-----------|-----|
| Laser Doppler imaging (LDI) | Blood flow measurement | Predicts healing at 48-72 hrs |
| Indocyanine green angiography | Perfusion mapping | Intraoperative assessment |
| Thermography | Temperature difference | Research applications |

**Laser Doppler Imaging:**
- Most validated objective method
- Best performed 48-72 hours post-burn
- Color-coded perfusion maps
- Predicts healing time with >95% accuracy
- High flow (red) = superficial, will heal
- Low flow (blue) = deep, needs grafting

## Burn Evolution and Zones of Injury

### Jackson Zones of Burn Injury
| Zone | Description | Fate |
|------|-------------|------|
| Zone of Coagulation | Central, irreversible necrosis | Cannot be salvaged |
| Zone of Stasis | Surrounding area, impaired perfusion | May convert to necrosis or recover |
| Zone of Hyperemia | Outermost, increased blood flow | Will recover |

### Factors Causing Burn Conversion
| Factor | Mechanism |
|--------|-----------|
| Inadequate resuscitation | Hypoperfusion of zone of stasis |
| Infection | Bacterial invasion, toxins |
| Edema | Increased tissue pressure |
| Desiccation | Tissue death from drying |
| Repeated trauma | Dressing changes, manipulation |
| Vasoactive substances | Catecholamines, vasopressors |

### Preventing Burn Conversion
- Adequate fluid resuscitation
- Wound care to prevent infection
- Maintain moisture balance
- Avoid excessive manipulation
- Minimize vasoconstrictor use
- Maintain normothermia

## Special Burn Types

### Chemical Burns
| Agent | Mechanism | Treatment |
|-------|-----------|-----------|
| Acids | Coagulation necrosis | Copious water irrigation |
| Alkalis | Liquefaction necrosis (deeper) | Prolonged irrigation (hours) |
| Hydrofluoric acid | Calcium/magnesium binding, systemic toxicity | Calcium gluconate gel, possible IV calcium |
| Phenol | Protein denaturation | Polyethylene glycol (PEG) or water |
| White phosphorus | Ignites when dry | Keep moist, remove particles |

### Electrical Burns
| Type | Voltage | Characteristics |
|------|---------|-----------------|
| Low voltage | <1000V | Entry/exit wounds, local injury |
| High voltage | >1000V | Extensive deep tissue injury, cardiac arrhythmias |
| Arc/Flash | Variable | Surface burns from arc heat |
| Lightning | Very high | Flashover effect, cardiac arrest |

**Electrical Burn Considerations:**
- Surface injury underestimates deep damage
- Muscle necrosis (rhabdomyolysis)
- Compartment syndrome risk
- Cardiac monitoring required
- High urine output targets (1-1.5 mL/kg/hr)
- Serial CK levels
- Fasciotomy may be required

### Radiation Burns
| Phase | Timing | Manifestation |
|-------|--------|---------------|
| Prodromal | Hours | Erythema, edema |
| Latent | Days to weeks | Apparent recovery |
| Manifest illness | Weeks | Desquamation, ulceration |
| Late effects | Months to years | Fibrosis, malignancy |`,
