        title: 'Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) Calculation',
        content: `# Calculating Burn Size (TBSA)

Accurate TBSA calculation is critical for determining fluid resuscitation requirements, transfer criteria, prognosis, and treatment planning.

## Rule of Nines (Adult)

### Standard Adult Percentages
| Body Region | Percentage |
|-------------|------------|
| Head and neck | 9% |
| Each upper extremity | 9% (anterior 4.5%, posterior 4.5%) |
| Anterior trunk | 18% |
| Posterior trunk | 18% |
| Each lower extremity | 18% (anterior 9%, posterior 9%) |
| Perineum/genitalia | 1% |
| **Total** | **100%** |

### Regional Breakdown
| Region | Anterior | Posterior |
|--------|----------|-----------|
| Head | 4.5% | 4.5% |
| Neck | Included | Included |
| Chest | 9% | - |
| Abdomen | 9% | - |
| Upper back | - | 9% |
| Lower back/buttocks | - | 9% |
| Each arm | 4.5% | 4.5% |
| Each hand | 2.5% (palm ~1%) | - |
| Each thigh | 4.5% | 4.5% |
| Each lower leg | 4.5% | 4.5% |
| Each foot | Included | Included |

## Pediatric TBSA Calculation

### Lund-Browder Chart
Most accurate method - accounts for age-related body proportion changes

| Body Part | Birth | 1 yr | 5 yr | 10 yr | 15 yr | Adult |
|-----------|-------|------|------|-------|-------|-------|
| Head | 19% | 17% | 13% | 11% | 9% | 7% |
| Neck | 2% | 2% | 2% | 2% | 2% | 2% |
| Anterior trunk | 13% | 13% | 13% | 13% | 13% | 13% |
| Posterior trunk | 13% | 13% | 13% | 13% | 13% | 13% |
| Each buttock | 2.5% | 2.5% | 2.5% | 2.5% | 2.5% | 2.5% |
| Genitalia | 1% | 1% | 1% | 1% | 1% | 1% |
| Each upper arm | 4% | 4% | 4% | 4% | 4% | 4% |
| Each lower arm | 3% | 3% | 3% | 3% | 3% | 3% |
| Each hand | 2.5% | 2.5% | 2.5% | 2.5% | 2.5% | 2.5% |
| Each thigh | 5.5% | 6.5% | 8% | 8.5% | 9% | 9.5% |
| Each lower leg | 5% | 5% | 5.5% | 6% | 6.5% | 7% |
| Each foot | 3.5% | 3.5% | 3.5% | 3.5% | 3.5% | 3.5% |

### Key Pediatric Differences
| Age Group | Head | Each Leg |
|-----------|------|----------|
| Infant (0-1 yr) | 18-19% | 13-14% |
| Child (5 yr) | 13% | 16% |
| Adolescent (15 yr) | 9% | 18% |

**Remember:** As head size decreases proportionally with age, leg size increases proportionally.

## Palm Method

### Technique
| Method | Measurement | Use |
|--------|-------------|-----|
| Palm only | ~0.5% TBSA | Scattered small burns |
| Palm + fingers | ~1% TBSA | Quick estimation |

### Best Applications
- Scattered burns
- Small burns
- Field/initial assessment
- Complementary to Rule of Nines for irregular patterns

## Burn Mapping and Documentation

### Standardized Burn Diagram
- Mark burned areas on body diagram
- Distinguish burn depths with different colors/patterns:
  - Superficial partial: Light shading
  - Deep partial: Medium shading
  - Full thickness: Dark/solid shading
- Calculate TBSA for each depth category
- Update as burns evolve

### Documentation Requirements
| Element | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| Total TBSA | Sum of all partial and full-thickness areas |
| TBSA by depth | Superficial partial %, Deep partial %, Full-thickness % |
| Distribution | Anterior/posterior, regional |
| Special areas | Face, hands, feet, perineum, joints |
| Circumferential | Yes/No and location |

## Common Errors in TBSA Estimation

### Sources of Error
| Error Type | Consequence |
|------------|-------------|
| Including superficial burns | Overestimation, fluid overload |
| Overlapping estimates | Double counting areas |
| Failing to reassess | Missing evolving burns |
| Using adult Rule of Nines for children | Significant inaccuracy |
| Estimating in field without re-evaluation | Persistent errors |

### Tips for Accuracy
- Use Lund-Browder for all pediatric patients
- Re-estimate TBSA after wound cleaning
- Have second provider verify estimation
- Update TBSA as burns demarcate at 48-72 hours
- Dont forget posterior surfaces

## TBSA and Clinical Decision Making

### Resuscitation Thresholds
| TBSA Threshold | Clinical Significance |
|----------------|----------------------|
| >10% (children) | Requires IV fluid resuscitation |
| >15-20% (adults) | Requires IV fluid resuscitation |
| >20% | Burn center referral recommended |
| >40% | Major burn, high mortality risk |
| >60% | Critical burn, very high mortality |

### Transfer Criteria to Burn Center (ABA)
| Criterion | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| TBSA | Partial-thickness >10% |
| Full-thickness | Any full-thickness burn |
| Special areas | Face, hands, feet, genitalia, perineum, major joints |
| Electrical burns | Including lightning |
| Chemical burns | Significant |
| Inhalation injury | Suspected or confirmed |
| Comorbidities | Preexisting conditions complicating care |
| Associated trauma | When burn is greatest risk |
| Special populations | Children, elderly in non-specialized facilities |
| Social/rehab needs | Abuse, extensive rehabilitation required |`,
