        content: `# Burn Resuscitation Protocols

Adequate fluid resuscitation is the cornerstone of acute burn management, preventing burn shock and multi-organ failure while avoiding complications of over-resuscitation.

## Pathophysiology of Burn Shock

### Capillary Leak Phenomenon
| Time Period | Mechanism | Clinical Effect |
|-------------|-----------|-----------------|
| 0-8 hours | Maximal capillary permeability | Greatest fluid requirements |
| 8-24 hours | Decreasing permeability | Reducing fluid needs |
| 24-48 hours | Capillary integrity restored | Colloids retained intravascularly |

### Hemodynamic Changes
| Parameter | Change | Mechanism |
|-----------|--------|-----------|
| Cardiac output | Decreased | Hypovolemia, myocardial depressant factors |
| SVR | Increased | Catecholamine release, vasoconstriction |
| Intravascular volume | Decreased | Capillary leak, third spacing |
| Hematocrit | Elevated | Plasma loss > RBC loss |

## Resuscitation Formulas

### Parkland (Baxter) Formula - Most Widely Used
| Component | Calculation |
|-----------|-------------|
| Total 24-hour volume | 4 mL × body weight (kg) × %TBSA |
| First 8 hours | 50% of calculated volume |
| Next 16 hours | Remaining 50% |
| Fluid type | Lactated Ringers solution |

**Example Calculation:**
- 70 kg patient with 40% TBSA burn
- Total = 4 × 70 × 40 = **11,200 mL in 24 hours**
- First 8 hours: 5,600 mL = **700 mL/hr**
- Next 16 hours: 5,600 mL = **350 mL/hr**

### Modified Brooke Formula
| Component | Calculation |
|-----------|-------------|
| Total 24-hour volume | 2 mL × body weight (kg) × %TBSA |
| Distribution | Same as Parkland (50%/50%) |
| Advantage | Lower volumes, less edema |

### Comparison of Formulas
| Formula | Volume (mL/kg/%TBSA) | Notes |
|---------|---------------------|-------|
| Parkland | 4 mL | Most common, tends to over-resuscitate |
| Modified Brooke | 2 mL | Lower volume, military use |
| Consensus Formula | 2-4 mL | ABA recommendation, titrate to response |
| Rule of Tens (field) | %TBSA × 10 mL/hr | Simplified, initial estimate |

### Pediatric Resuscitation
| Formula | Volume | Notes |
|---------|--------|-------|
| Parkland | 4 mL/kg/%TBSA | Plus maintenance fluids |
| Maintenance | D5 1/2NS | Add to resuscitation volume |

**Pediatric Maintenance Fluid (4-2-1 rule):**
- First 10 kg: 4 mL/kg/hr
- Second 10 kg: 2 mL/kg/hr
- Each additional kg: 1 mL/kg/hr

## Critical Timing Considerations

### When to Start the Clock
| Scenario | Start Time |
|----------|------------|
| Known injury time | Time of burn |
| Unknown time | Estimated based on history |
| Delayed presentation | Calculate deficit, catch up over remaining time |

**Example - Delayed Presentation:**
- 60 kg patient, 30% TBSA, arrives 4 hours post-burn
- Total 24h fluid: 4 × 60 × 30 = 7,200 mL
- First 8 hours: 3,600 mL
- 4 hours elapsed, 4 hours remaining: Give 3,600 mL in 4 hours = 900 mL/hr

## Monitoring Resuscitation

### Target Parameters
| Parameter | Adult Target | Pediatric Target | Notes |
|-----------|--------------|------------------|-------|
| Urine output | 0.5-1 mL/kg/hr (30-50 mL/hr) | 1-1.5 mL/kg/hr | Primary endpoint |
| MAP | >65 mmHg | Age-appropriate | Avoid vasopressors if possible |
| Heart rate | <120 bpm | Age-appropriate | Decreasing with resuscitation |
| Mental status | Alert, oriented | Age-appropriate | Reflects perfusion |
| Base deficit | Improving | Improving | <6 indicates adequate resuscitation |
| Lactate | <2 mmol/L | <2 mmol/L | Decreasing trend |

### Special Situations - Increased Fluid Needs
| Situation | Adjustment |
|-----------|------------|
| Inhalation injury | Increase by 40-50% |
| Electrical burns | Target UO 1-1.5 mL/kg/hr |
| Delayed resuscitation | Catch up carefully |
| Myoglobinuria | Target UO 1-2 mL/kg/hr |
| Alcohol intoxication | May need more fluid |

### Special Situations - Decreased Fluid Needs
| Situation | Adjustment |
|-----------|------------|
| Elderly | More careful monitoring |
| Cardiac disease | Watch for overload |
| Renal disease | Adjust based on output |

## Second 24 Hours (Day 2)

### Colloid Resuscitation
| Component | Amount |
|-----------|--------|
| Colloid (5% albumin) | 0.3-0.5 mL/kg/%TBSA |
| Crystalloid | Maintain UO |
| Dextrose | Add D5W for free water |

### Goals
- Maintain urine output
- Begin diuresis of resuscitation fluid
- Transition to enteral nutrition
- Reduce IV fluids as tolerated

## Complications of Resuscitation

### Over-Resuscitation (Fluid Creep)
| Complication | Mechanism | Prevention/Treatment |
|--------------|-----------|---------------------|
| Pulmonary edema | Capillary leak + excess fluid | Titrate to UO, consider colloids |
| Abdominal compartment syndrome | Third spacing, bowel edema | Measure IAP, decompress if >25 mmHg |
| Extremity compartment syndrome | Tissue edema | Escharotomy, fasciotomy |
| Orbital compartment syndrome | Facial/periorbital edema | Lateral canthotomy |
| Cerebral edema | Increased ICP | Judicious resuscitation |

### Under-Resuscitation
| Complication | Consequence |
|--------------|-------------|
| Acute kidney injury | Oliguria, rising creatinine |
| Burn wound conversion | Zone of stasis necrosis |
| Multi-organ failure | Inadequate perfusion |
| Death | Hypovolemic shock |

### Abdominal Compartment Syndrome
| Grade | Intra-abdominal Pressure | Management |
|-------|-------------------------|------------|
| I | 12-15 mmHg | Optimize resuscitation |
| II | 16-20 mmHg | Consider NG decompression, diuretics |
| III | 21-25 mmHg | Aggressive management, consider decompression |
| IV | >25 mmHg | Decompressive laparotomy |

## Adjuncts to Resuscitation

### Vitamin C (Ascorbic Acid) - High Dose Protocol
| Parameter | Details |
|-----------|---------|
| Dose | 66 mg/kg/hr for first 24 hours |
| Mechanism | Antioxidant, reduces capillary leak |
| Evidence | Mixed - reduces fluid requirements in some studies |
| Caution | Oxalate nephropathy risk at high doses |

### Plasma Resuscitation
| Indication | Rationale |
|------------|-----------|
| Large burns (>40% TBSA) | Replaces clotting factors |
| Coagulopathy | Corrects INR |
| Reduces crystalloid | Lower total fluid volume |`,
