        title: 'Flexor Tendon Injuries',
        content: `# Flexor Tendon Injuries: Comprehensive Management

## Anatomy Review

### Flexor Tendons to Digits
| Tendon | Origin | Insertion | Function |
|--------|--------|-----------|----------|
| FDS | Medial epicondyle, radius, ulna | Middle phalanx (split) | Flex PIP |
| FDP | Ulna, interosseous membrane | Distal phalanx base | Flex DIP |

### Thumb Flexors
| Tendon | Origin | Insertion |
|--------|--------|-----------|
| FPL | Radius | Distal phalanx base |
| FPB | Trapezium, flexor retinaculum | Proximal phalanx |

### Blood Supply to Flexor Tendons
| Source | Location | Clinical Relevance |
|--------|----------|-------------------|
| Vincula brevia | Near insertion | Short vinculum |
| Vincula longa | Proximal segment | Long vinculum |
| Synovial diffusion | Throughout | Nutrition in sheath |
| Segmental vessels | Volar surface | Handle tendons carefully |

## Clinical Examination

### Testing FDP Function
| Step | Technique |
|------|-----------|
| 1. Position | Hold MCP and PIP extended |
| 2. Isolation | Stabilize middle phalanx |
| 3. Command | Ask to flex DIP |
| 4. Result | Intact if DIP flexes actively |

### Testing FDS Function
| Step | Technique |
|------|-----------|
| 1. Position | Hold all OTHER fingers in extension |
| 2. Rationale | Blocks FDP (quadriga effect) |
| 3. Command | Ask to flex tested finger at PIP |
| 4. Result | Intact if PIP flexes independently |
| Exception | Small finger FDS absent in 15-20% |

### Key Physical Exam Findings
| Finding | Indicates |
|---------|-----------|
| Finger in extension | Complete laceration |
| Loss of cascade | Tendon disruption |
| Unable to flex DIP | FDP laceration |
| Unable to flex PIP (isolated) | FDS laceration |
| Weakness without complete loss | Partial laceration |

## Classification of Injuries

### By Zone (Fingers)
| Zone | Boundaries | Contents | Prognosis |
|------|------------|----------|-----------|
| I | FDS insertion to DIP | FDP only | Good |
| II | A1 to FDS insertion | FDS + FDP | Most difficult |
| III | Distal crease to A1 | Lumbricals | Good |
| IV | Carpal tunnel | 9 tendons | Adhesion risk |
| V | Proximal to tunnel | Muscle | Best |

### By Zone (Thumb)
| Zone | Location |
|------|----------|
| T1 | Distal to IP |
| T2 | A1 to IP |
| T3 | Thenar eminence |

### Jersey Finger (FDP Avulsion)

#### Mechanism
- Forceful extension against flexed DIP
- Ring finger: 75% of cases (weakest FDP insertion)
- Sports injury: grabbing jersey

#### Leddy Classification
| Type | Retraction Level | Vinculum | Timing |
|------|-----------------|----------|--------|
| I | Palm | Disrupted | Emergency (<7-10 days) |
| II | PIP level | Intact | Repair within weeks |
| III | Distal phalanx (bony) | Intact | Can delay |
| IV | Type III + distal avulsion | Variable | May need ORIF |
| V | Comminuted distal phalanx | Variable | Complex reconstruction |

### Partial Lacerations
| Extent | Management |
|--------|------------|
| <25% | Trim ragged edges |
| 25-50% | Epitendinous repair only |
| >50% | Full core + epitendinous |
| >75% | Treat as complete |

## Surgical Repair Principles

### Timing
| Category | Time | Outcome |
|----------|------|---------|
| Primary | <24 hours | Ideal |
| Delayed primary | 1-10 days | Acceptable |
| Secondary | 10 days - 4 weeks | Difficult (edema, scarring) |
| Late | >4 weeks | Staged reconstruction |

### Core Suture Techniques
| Technique | Strands | Strength | Notes |
|-----------|---------|----------|-------|
| Modified Kessler | 2 | ~20N | Simple, historical |
| Cruciate | 4 | ~40N | Common current choice |
| Savage | 6 | ~60N | Strongest |
| Adelaide | 4 | ~40N | Low profile |
| Tang | 6 | ~60N | Multi-strand |

### Strength Principles
| Concept | Details |
|---------|---------|
| Per-strand strength | ~10N per core strand |
| Minimum for early motion | 40N (4-strand) |
| Optimal | 50-60N (6-strand) |
| Gap force | ~5mm gap causes 50% strength loss |
| Epitendinous contribution | Adds 10-50% strength |

### Suture Material
| Material | Properties |
|----------|------------|
| 3-0 or 4-0 braided | Core suture |
| 5-0 or 6-0 monofilament | Epitendinous |
| Non-absorbable | Standard choice |
| Absorbable | May be used, loses strength |

### Epitendinous (Running Peripheral) Suture
| Purpose | Benefit |
|---------|---------|
| Smooth repair surface | Improves gliding |
| Adds strength | 10-50% additional |
| Reduces gapping | Distributes stress |
| Technique | Simple running or horizontal mattress |

### Surgical Principles
| Principle | Rationale |
|-----------|-----------|
| Handle tendons gently | Preserve blood supply |
| Minimum suture passes | Reduce strangulation |
| Lock suture configuration | Prevents gapping |
| Secure knots | Bury away from repair surface |
| Avoid shortening | Maintain tendon length |
| Test gliding | Ensure no impingement |

## Pulley Management

### Critical Pulleys
| Pulley | Why Critical |
|--------|--------------|
| A2 | Prevents bowstringing over PP |
| A4 | Prevents bowstringing over MP |

### Pulley Preservation Rules
| Rule | Details |
|------|---------|
| Preserve A2 + A4 | Always |
| Can release A1, A3, A5 | If needed for exposure/motion |
| Partial release | If full A2/A4 cannot be preserved |
| Minimum to retain | One intact + 3cm second pulley |

### Pulley Reconstruction (if needed)
| Technique | Graft Source |
|-----------|--------------|
| Loop technique | Palmaris longus |
| Belt loop | FDS slip |
| Wrap around | Extensor retinaculum |

## Rehabilitation Protocols

### Early Active Motion (Current Standard)
| Phase | Protocol |
|-------|----------|
| Week 1-2 | Place and hold exercises |
| Week 2-4 | Protected active flexion |
| Week 4-6 | Light resistance, tendon gliding |
| Week 6-8 | Progressive strengthening |
| Week 8+ | Full activity |

### Place and Hold Technique
| Step | Description |
|------|-------------|
| 1 | Passively flex to full fist |
| 2 | Patient maintains position (holds) |
| 3 | Slowly extends (active) |
| Purpose | Minimal stress on repair |

### Duran Protocol (Controlled Passive Motion)
| Phase | Protocol |
|-------|----------|
| Weeks 0-4 | Passive motion only (therapist) |
| Weeks 4-6 | Active motion begins |
| Indication | Weaker repairs, questionable compliance |
| Disadvantage | Higher adhesion rates |

### Kleinert Protocol (Dynamic Flexion)
| Feature | Details |
|---------|---------|
| Rubber band traction | Passive flexion |
| Active extension | Against rubber band |
| Risk | Flexion contracture, DIP lag |

## Complications

### Adhesions
| Risk Factor | Prevention |
|-------------|------------|
| Zone II | Meticulous technique, early motion |
| Crush injury | Staged reconstruction |
| Immobilization | Early protected motion |
| Treatment | Tenolysis (wait 6-12 months) |

### Rupture
| Risk Factor | Prevention |
|-------------|------------|
| Weak repair | 4+ strand technique |
| Early loading | Compliance with therapy |
| Gap at repair | Proper surgical technique |
| Incidence | 3-10% |

### Other Complications
| Complication | Management |
|--------------|------------|
| Bowstringing | Pulley reconstruction |
| Contracture | Therapy, possible release |
| Quadriga | Limit advancement to 1cm |
| Lumbrical plus | Address FDP-lumbrical relationship |

## Special Situations

### Staged Flexor Tendon Reconstruction
| Indication | Approach |
|------------|----------|
| Failed primary repair | Staged |
| Severe scarring | Staged |
| Pulley loss | Staged |

| Stage | Procedure |
|-------|-----------|
| 1 | Silicone rod placement, pulley reconstruction |
| Wait | 2-3 months for sheath formation |
| 2 | Remove rod, place tendon graft |

### Tendon Grafts
| Donor | Features |
|-------|----------|
| Palmaris longus | First choice, easily accessible |
| Plantaris | Good alternative |
| Toe extensors | If others unavailable |
| Long toe flexors | Larger caliber |

### Tendon Transfer Options
| Indication | Transfer |
|------------|----------|
| Irreparable FDP | FDS to FDP |
| Multiple tendon loss | Consider tenodesis |`,
