        id: 'hand-5',
        title: 'Syndactyly and Congenital Hand Anomalies',
        content: `## Congenital Hand Differences

### Classification (Swanson/IFSSH)

**I. Failure of Formation:**
- Transverse (congenital amputation)
- Longitudinal (radial/ulnar deficiency, cleft hand)

**II. Failure of Differentiation:**
- Syndactyly (simple/complex, complete/incomplete)
- Symphalangism
- Camptodactyly
- Clinodactyly

**III. Duplication:**
- Polydactyly (preaxial, central, postaxial)

**IV. Overgrowth:**
- Macrodactyly

**V. Undergrowth:**
- Brachydactyly
- Thumb hypoplasia

**VI. Constriction Ring Syndrome:**
- Amniotic band sequence

**VII. Generalized Skeletal Abnormalities**

### Syndactyly

**Types:**
- Simple: Skin only
- Complex: Bone fusion
- Complete: To fingertip
- Incomplete: Partial fusion
- Complicated: Accessory structures

**Surgical Timing:**
- Simple syndactyly: 12-18 months
- Border digits (thumb-index, ring-small): 6 months (growth discrepancy)
- Complex syndactyly: Case-by-case

**Surgical Principles:**
- Never release adjacent web spaces simultaneously
- Interdigitating flaps for web reconstruction
- Full-thickness skin grafts for defects
- Z-plasty flaps prevent scar contracture

**Web Space Reconstruction:**
- Dorsal rectangular flap (most common)
- Butterfly flap
- Interdigitating triangular flaps

### Polydactyly

**Preaxial (Radial/Thumb):**
- Wassel Classification (Types I-VII)
- Type IV most common (duplicated proximal phalanx)
- Treatment: Reconstruct one functional thumb

**Postaxial (Ulnar):**
- Type A: Well-formed digit
- Type B: Rudimentary/pedunculated
- More common in African descent
- Pedunculated: Suture ligation at birth

**Central:**
- Rare
- Often associated with syndactyly (polysyndactyly)

### Thumb Hypoplasia (Blauth Classification)

**Type I:** Minor hypoplasia - observe

**Type II:** Narrow first web, thenar hypoplasia
- Web deepening + opponensplasty

**Type III:** Same as II + skeletal abnormalities
- IIIA: Stable CMC - reconstruct
- IIIB: Unstable CMC - pollicization

**Type IV:** Floating thumb - pollicization

**Type V:** Absent thumb - pollicization`,
