        content: `# Microsurgery: Principles and Techniques

## Introduction and History

### Evolution of Microsurgery
| Year | Milestone |
|------|-----------|
| 1960 | Jacobson & Suarez - first microvascular anastomosis |
| 1962 | Malt - first successful replantation (arm) |
| 1965 | Komatsu & Tamai - first digit replantation |
| 1972 | Daniel & Taylor - first free flap for trauma |
| 1973 | Harii - first free groin flap for head/neck |

### Definition and Scope
| Application | Examples |
|-------------|----------|
| Vessel anastomosis | 0.3-3mm diameter |
| Nerve repair | Digital, brachial plexus |
| Lymphatic surgery | Lymphovenous bypass |
| Replantation | Digits, hands, scalp |
| Free tissue transfer | DIEP, fibula, radial forearm |

## Operating Room Setup

### Room Environment
| Factor | Optimal Setting |
|--------|----------------|
| Temperature | 72-76°F (22-24°C) |
| Humidity | 50-60% |
| Traffic | Minimal |
| Positioning | Comfortable for 6+ hours |
| Lighting | Adequate but not interfering with scope |

### Team Requirements
| Role | Responsibility |
|------|----------------|
| Primary surgeon | Anastomosis, flap inset |
| Assistant surgeon | Counter-traction, flap harvest |
| Scrub nurse | Microsurgery-trained |
| Circulator | Minimize traffic |
| Anesthesia | Stable hemodynamics, warmth |

## Essential Equipment

### Microscope
| Feature | Specification |
|---------|--------------|
| Magnification | 6-40x variable |
| Illumination | Coaxial, bright |
| Viewing | Dual eyepieces (surgeon/assistant) |
| Controls | Foot-operated zoom/focus |
| Balance | Counterweighted for positioning |
| Drapes | Sterile covers |

### Magnification Options
| Type | Magnification | Use |
|------|---------------|-----|
| Loupes | 2.5-6x | Flap harvest, large vessels |
| Operating microscope | 6-40x | Anastomosis |
| Typical anastomosis | 10-16x | Most microsurgery |
| Fine work | 20-40x | Small vessels, nerves |

### Instruments
| Instrument | Use |
|------------|-----|
| Jeweler's forceps (#3, #5) | Tissue handling |
| Micro needle holders | Suturing |
| Micro scissors (straight) | Cutting sutures |
| Micro scissors (curved) | Adventitia, vessel prep |
| Vessel dilators | Gently open vessel lumen |
| Double micro clamps | Vessel approximation |
| Single clamps | Background control |
| Irrigating cannula | Heparinized saline |
| Background material | Blue/green silicone sheet |

### Suture Selection by Vessel Size
| Vessel Diameter | Suture Size | Examples |
|-----------------|-------------|----------|
| >3mm | 8-0 nylon | Radial artery, large veins |
| 1.5-3mm | 9-0 nylon | Digital arteries, most flaps |
| 1-1.5mm | 10-0 nylon | Small digital vessels |
| <1mm | 11-0 nylon | Fingertip replants |

### Suture Characteristics
| Property | Preference |
|----------|------------|
| Material | Nylon (non-reactive, smooth) |
| Needle | Tapered, round body |
| Needle size | 50-150 microns |
| Suture diameter | 20-50 microns |
| Color | Black (visible against tissue) |

## Vessel Preparation

### Dissection Principles
| Principle | Technique |
|-----------|-----------|
| Exposure | Adequate but minimal trauma |
| Isolation | Identify vessel clearly |
| Branches | Ligate/clip before dividing |
| Length | Adequate for tension-free repair |
| Handling | Adventitia only |

### Adventitial Stripping
| Step | Details |
|------|---------|
| Purpose | Remove thrombogenic adventitia |
| Length | 1-2mm from cut end |
| Technique | Grasp, strip with micro scissors |
| Caution | Avoid intimal damage |
| Importance | Critical for patency |

### Vessel Dilation
| Indication | Technique |
|------------|-----------|
| Vasospasm | Gentle mechanical dilation |
| Preparation | Open lumen for suturing |
| Tools | Smooth vessel dilators |
| Pharmacologic | Papaverine, lidocaine topically |

### Irrigation
| Solution | Concentration | Purpose |
|----------|---------------|---------|
| Heparinized saline | 100 units/mL | Prevent clotting |
| Papaverine | 30mg/mL | Vasodilation |
| Lidocaine | 1% | Vasodilation |
| Application | Frequent, gentle irrigation |

## Clamping

### Clamp Types
| Type | Use |
|------|-----|
| Approximating (double) | Bring vessel ends together |
| Single clamps | Background vessels |
| Acland clamps | Most common, color-coded |
| Vessel loops | Alternative for larger vessels |

### Clamp Application
| Principle | Rationale |
|-----------|-----------|
| Minimal pressure | Prevent intimal damage |
| Appropriate size | Match vessel diameter |
| Adequate distance | Allow vessel preparation |
| Correct orientation | Easy anastomosis access |

### Ischemia Considerations
| Tissue | Safe Ischemia Time |
|--------|-------------------|
| Muscle | 4-6 hours |
| Skin | 6-8 hours |
| Bone | 6-12 hours |
| Digit (cooled) | 12-24 hours |
| Cooling | Prolongs safe ischemia |

## Basic Suturing Technique

### Key Principles
| Principle | Technique |
|-----------|-----------|
| Gentle handling | Touch adventitia only |
| Perpendicular entry | 90° to vessel wall |
| Equal bites | Same depth both sides |
| Full thickness | Through all layers |
| Edge eversion | Slight eversion preferred |
| Spacing | 0.5mm (small vessels) |

### Knot Tying
| Step | Details |
|------|---------|
| First throw | Square knot |
| Tension | Approximate, don't strangulate |
| Throws | Minimum 3 (nylon slippery) |
| Cut length | 1-2mm tails |
| Placement | Knots outside vessel |

### Common Errors
| Error | Consequence |
|-------|-------------|
| Adventitia in lumen | Thrombosis |
| Intimal damage | Thrombosis |
| Too tight | Vessel stenosis |
| Too loose | Leak |
| Uneven bites | Poor coaptation |
| Back-wall sutures | Stenosis |

## Pharmacological Adjuncts

### Antispasmodics
| Agent | Mechanism | Application |
|-------|-----------|-------------|
| Papaverine | Smooth muscle relaxation | Topical |
| Lidocaine | Local anesthetic vasodilation | Topical |
| Verapamil | Calcium channel blocker | Topical |
| Nitroglycerin | Nitric oxide donor | Topical |

### Anticoagulation
| Agent | Dose | Use |
|-------|------|-----|
| Heparin (systemic) | Varies | Not routine for elective flaps |
| Heparin (local) | 100 U/mL irrigation | Standard |
| Aspirin | 325mg daily | Often used post-op |
| Dextran | Controversial | Some centers use |

### Thrombolytics (Rescue)
| Agent | Indication |
|-------|------------|
| TPA (tissue plasminogen activator) | Salvage for thrombosis |
| Urokinase | Alternative |
| Dose | Low-dose local infusion |

## Postoperative Monitoring

### Clinical Signs
| Parameter | Normal | Concerning |
|-----------|--------|------------|
| Color | Pink | Pale (arterial), blue/dusky (venous) |
| Capillary refill | <2 seconds | Slow (arterial), brisk (venous) |
| Temperature | Warm | Cold |
| Turgor | Soft | Tense (venous congestion) |
| Bleeding on pin-prick | Red, moderate | None (arterial), dark/excessive (venous) |

### Monitoring Devices
| Device | Mechanism |
|--------|-----------|
| Implantable Doppler | Continuous arterial signal |
| External Doppler | Intermittent signal check |
| Tissue oximetry | SpO2 in tissue |
| Near-infrared spectroscopy | Tissue oxygenation |
| ICG angiography | Perfusion assessment |

### Monitoring Protocol
| Time | Frequency |
|------|-----------|
| First 24 hours | Every 1 hour |
| 24-48 hours | Every 2 hours |
| 48-72 hours | Every 4 hours |
| After 72 hours | Every 4-8 hours |`,
