        id: 'micro-2',
        title: 'Vascular Anastomosis Techniques',
        content: `# Microvascular Anastomosis: Technical Details

## Types of Anastomosis

### Overview
| Type | Indication | Advantages |
|------|------------|------------|
| End-to-end | Equal-sized vessels | Standard, reliable |
| End-to-side | Size mismatch, preserve flow | Maintains recipient flow |
| Interpositional graft | Gap, tension, revision | Bridges difficult situations |

## End-to-End Anastomosis

### Classical Triangulation Technique (Carrel)
| Step | Details |
|------|---------|
| 1. Stay sutures | Place 3 sutures 120° apart |
| 2. First segment | Complete sutures between stays (A to B) |
| 3. Rotate clamps | Expose second segment |
| 4. Second segment | Complete sutures (B to C) |
| 5. Rotate again | Expose third segment |
| 6. Third segment | Complete sutures (C to A) |

### 180° Back-Wall-First Technique
| Step | Details |
|------|---------|
| 1. Stay sutures | Place 2 sutures 180° apart |
| 2. Back wall first | Complete sutures on far wall |
| 3. Flip clamps | Rotate 180° |
| 4. Front wall | Complete remaining sutures |
| Advantage | Easier visualization of back wall |

### Parachute Technique
| Step | Details |
|------|---------|
| 1. | Place all sutures loosely (not tied) |
| 2. | "Parachute" recipient vessel down |
| 3. | Tie sutures sequentially |
| Use | Difficult access, deep vessels |

### Suture Placement Guidelines
| Parameter | Guideline |
|-----------|-----------|
| Bite depth | Equal to vessel wall thickness |
| Distance from edge | 1-2 wall thicknesses |
| Spacing | 0.5mm for small vessels |
| Number of sutures | 6-12 for typical anastomosis |
| Full thickness | All layers (intima to adventitia) |

### Achieving Edge Eversion
| Method | Details |
|--------|---------|
| Proper needle angle | 90° entry, exit at edge |
| Adequate bites | Full thickness |
| Proper tension | Approximate without strangulating |
| Visualization | Confirm intima-to-intima apposition |

## End-to-Side Anastomosis

### Indications
| Indication | Rationale |
|------------|-----------|
| Size discrepancy | Large recipient, small donor |
| Preserve recipient flow | Don't sacrifice main vessel |
| Avoid tension | Alternative position |
| Multiple anastomoses | T-configuration |

### Arteriotomy Creation
| Step | Details |
|------|---------|
| Location | Recipient vessel apex |
| Size | 1.5x diameter of donor vessel |
| Shape | Oval or fish-mouth |
| Technique | Blade, then scissors |
| Irrigation | Remove debris |

### Anastomosis Technique
| Step | Details |
|------|---------|
| 1. | Place stay sutures at apex and heel |
| 2. | Back wall first (inside-out or parachute) |
| 3. | Tie stay sutures |
| 4. | Complete front wall |
| 5. | Check for leaks |

### Angle of Entry
| Angle | Flow Characteristics |
|-------|---------------------|
| 90° | Turbulence, higher thrombosis |
| 30-45° | Optimal flow dynamics |
| Very acute | Risk of kinking |

## Venous Anastomosis

### Differences from Arterial
| Feature | Artery | Vein |
|---------|--------|------|
| Wall thickness | Thick | Thin |
| Elasticity | High | Low |
| Handling | Moderate | Delicate |
| Collapse | Minimal | Collapses easily |
| Thrombosis risk | Lower | Higher |

### Venous Coupler Device
| Feature | Details |
|---------|---------|
| Principle | Mechanical anastomosis |
| Technique | Evert vein over pins, close |
| Advantages | Faster, reliable, less skill-dependent |
| Limitations | End-to-end only, size range limited |
| Sizes | 1.5mm to 4mm |
| Success rate | Comparable to hand-sewn |

### Two Veins Per Artery
| Rationale | Details |
|-----------|---------|
| Redundancy | Backup if one thromboses |
| Flow | Matches arterial inflow |
| Congestion prevention | Reduces venous hypertension |
| Recommendation | Whenever feasible |

## Managing Size Discrepancy

### Mild Discrepancy (up to 2:1)
| Technique | Method |
|-----------|--------|
| Oblique cut | Angle larger vessel |
| Beveling | Increase circumference |
| Uneven sutures | More on larger side |
| Fishmouthing | Open smaller vessel |

### Moderate Discrepancy (2:1 to 3:1)
| Technique | Method |
|-----------|--------|
| End-to-side | Standard approach |
| Fishmouthing | Significant opening |
| Vein patch | Enlarge smaller vessel |

### Severe Discrepancy (>3:1)
| Technique | Method |
|-----------|--------|
| Interpositional graft | Bridge with vein |
| Alternative recipient | Find better-sized vessel |
| Branch point anastomosis | Use Y-configuration |

## Testing Patency

### Empty-and-Refill Test
| Step | Details |
|------|---------|
| 1. | Occlude distal to anastomosis |
| 2. | "Milk" blood proximally with forceps |
| 3. | Release distal occlusion |
| 4. | Observe for brisk refill |

### Interpretation
| Finding | Meaning |
|---------|---------|
| Brisk pulsatile refill | Patent anastomosis |
| No refill | Technical error, spasm, or thrombosis |
| Slow refill | Partial obstruction, stenosis |
| Sluggish pulsation | May be acceptable |

### Flicker Test
| Step | Details |
|------|---------|
| 1. | Observe anastomosis without clamps |
| 2. | Look for pulsatile "flicker" at suture line |
| 3. | Indicates flow through vessel |

### Additional Tests
| Test | Use |
|------|-----|
| Doppler probe | Audible arterial signal |
| ICG angiography | Visual perfusion assessment |
| Strip test | Milk blood out, watch refill |

## Troubleshooting Problems

### Vasospasm
| Treatment | Details |
|-----------|---------|
| Papaverine | Topical, 30mg/mL |
| Lidocaine | Topical, 1% |
| Warm saline | Irrigation |
| Gentle dilation | Mechanical |
| Time/patience | Often resolves |

### Thrombosis
| Step | Action |
|------|--------|
| 1. | Remove clamps, extract clot with forceps |
| 2. | Irrigate with heparinized saline |
| 3. | Re-examine for technical error |
| 4. | Resect anastomosis, redo if needed |
| 5. | Consider thrombolytics (TPA) |

### Leaks
| Location | Solution |
|----------|----------|
| Minor (pinhole) | Additional suture |
| Between sutures | Add interrupted sutures |
| Major | Revise anastomosis |
| Caution | Too many sutures → stenosis |

### Technical Errors Requiring Revision
| Error | Action |
|-------|--------|
| Back-wall catch | Must take down and redo |
| Significant stenosis | Revise |
| Persistent thrombosis | Resect, graft if needed |
| Vessel damage | Consider graft |

## Vein Grafts

### Indications
| Situation | Rationale |
|-----------|-----------|
| Vessel gap | Cannot achieve tension-free |
| Zone of injury | Bridge traumatized segment |
| Revision | After thrombosed anastomosis |
| Size match | Achieve optimal caliber |

### Common Donor Veins
| Vein | Location | Size |
|------|----------|------|
| Greater saphenous | Leg (groin or ankle) | 3-5mm |
| Lesser saphenous | Posterior calf | 2-3mm |
| Cephalic vein | Arm, wrist | 2-3mm |
| Basilic vein | Arm | 2-4mm |
| Volar forearm veins | Wrist | 1-2mm |

### Graft Orientation
| Direction | Effect |
|-----------|--------|
| Reversed | Valves open in flow direction (standard for arterial) |
| Non-reversed | For venous reconstruction |
| Mark | Note orientation before division |

### Graft Handling
| Principle | Rationale |
|-----------|-----------|
| Gentle distension | Test for leaks, open lumen |
| Avoid overdistension | Damages endothelium |
| Keep moist | Prevent drying |
| Appropriate length | Avoid redundancy or tension |

## Special Situations

### Replantation Anastomosis
| Consideration | Details |
|---------------|---------|
| Vessel injury | Often crushed, avulsed |
| Multiple levels | May need graft |
| Sequence | Bone → tendon → artery → nerve → vein |
| Ischemia time | Factor in urgency |

### Radiated Field
| Challenge | Approach |
|-----------|----------|
| Fibrosis | More dissection needed |
| Vessel fragility | Gentle handling |
| Increased failure | Use recipient vessels outside XRT field |

### Redo Anastomosis
| Step | Details |
|------|---------|
| Remove failed anastomosis | Cut back to healthy vessel |
| Evaluate vessels | Check for intimal damage |
| Fresh ends | Re-prepare vessels |
| Consider graft | If length inadequate |`,
