        id: 'flap-4',
        title: 'Common Pedicled Flaps',
        content: `## Regional and Distant Pedicled Flaps

### Latissimus Dorsi Flap

**Classification:** Type V muscle (dominant + segmental)
**Pedicle:** Thoracodorsal artery (from subscapular)
**Dimensions:** Up to 25 x 40 cm

**Uses:**
- Breast reconstruction
- Chest wall defects
- Upper extremity coverage
- Head and neck (pedicled reach)

**Advantages:**
- Large, reliable
- Can include skin paddle
- Minimal donor morbidity

### Pectoralis Major Flap

**Classification:** Type V muscle
**Pedicle:** Thoracoacromial artery (dominant), internal mammary perforators
**Arc of Rotation:** Reaches oral cavity, skull base

**Uses:**
- Head and neck reconstruction (workhorse)
- Pharyngeal/esophageal defects
- Chest wall coverage

### Rectus Abdominis Flap

**Classification:** Type III muscle (2 dominant pedicles)
**Pedicles:** Superior epigastric, Deep inferior epigastric (DIEA)

**Variants:**
- **TRAM (pedicled):** Based on superior epigastric
- **TRAM (free):** Based on DIEA
- **VRAM:** Vertical skin paddle
- **DIEP (free):** Perforator only, spares muscle

**Uses:**
- Breast reconstruction (TRAM)
- Groin/perineal defects (VRAM)
- Lower extremity (free)

### Gracilis Flap

**Classification:** Type II muscle (dominant + minor)
**Pedicle:** Medial circumflex femoral (8 cm from pubic tubercle)

**Uses:**
- Perineal reconstruction
- Facial reanimation (free, functioning)
- Forearm coverage
- Vaginal reconstruction (split)

### Gastrocnemius Flap

**Classification:** Type I muscle (single pedicle)
**Pedicle:** Medial/Lateral sural arteries

**Uses:**
- Proximal tibia coverage
- Exposed knee prosthesis
- Popliteal fossa defects

**Notes:**
- Medial head larger, more commonly used
- Covers proximal 1/3 of tibia only`,
