        content: `## Post-Sternotomy Wound Infection

### Epidemiology

- Incidence: 1-5% after cardiac surgery
- Mortality: 10-40% if deep infection
- Risk increases with LIMA harvest

### Classification - Jones

| Type | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **Type I** | Superficial (skin/subcutaneous) |
| **Type II** | Deep, stable sternum |
| **Type III** | Deep, unstable sternum |
| **Type IV** | Septicemia |

### Risk Factors

**Patient Factors:**
- Diabetes mellitus
- Obesity (BMI >30)
- COPD
- Immunosuppression
- Renal failure
- Malnutrition

**Operative Factors:**
- Bilateral IMA harvest
- Prolonged CPB time
- Blood transfusion
- Re-exploration for bleeding
- Emergency surgery

### Microbiology

| Organism | Frequency |
|----------|-----------|
| Staphylococcus aureus | 30-40% |
| Coagulase-negative staph | 20-30% |
| Gram-negative | 10-20% |
| Mixed flora | Variable |
| MRSA | Increasing |

### Diagnosis

**Clinical Signs:**
- Wound erythema, drainage
- Sternal instability (click)
- Fever, elevated WBC
- Sternal dehiscence

**Imaging:**
- CT chest: Fluid, gas, bone erosion
- MRI: Soft tissue detail
- Nuclear scan: Osteomyelitis

### Initial Management

**Principles:**
1. Source control (debridement)
2. Appropriate antibiotics
3. Wound stabilization
4. Definitive soft tissue coverage

**Debridement:**
- Remove all necrotic tissue
- Remove infected wires
- Resect nonviable sternum
- Serial debridements if needed
- NPWT for temporization`,
