        id: 'hypo-1',
        title: 'Hypospadias: Anatomy and Classification',
        content: `## Understanding Hypospadias

### Definition

**Hypospadias:** Congenital condition where urethral meatus opens on the ventral surface of the penis, proximal to normal glanular position.

### Epidemiology

- Incidence: 1:200-300 male births
- Second most common male genital anomaly (after cryptorchidism)
- Increasing incidence (environmental endocrine disruptors?)

### Embryology

**Normal Development:**
- Urethral folds fuse ventrally (8-14 weeks)
- Fusion proceeds proximal to distal
- Prepuce develops circumferentially

**In Hypospadias:**
- Arrest of urethral fold fusion
- Deficient ventral tissue
- Hooded foreskin (dorsal only)

### Classification by Meatal Position

| Type | Location | Frequency |
|------|----------|-----------|
| **Anterior (Distal)** | Glanular, coronal, distal penile | 50-60% |
| **Middle** | Midshaft | 20-30% |
| **Posterior (Proximal)** | Proximal shaft, penoscrotal, scrotal, perineal | 15-20% |

### Associated Anomalies

**Chordee:**
- Ventral curvature of penis
- Present in 15-50%
- Caused by: fibrotic tissue, skin tethering, corporal disproportion

**Hooded Prepuce:**
- Dorsal foreskin only
- Absent ventral foreskin

**Cryptorchidism:**
- 10% of hypospadias patients
- Higher with proximal hypospadias

**Inguinal Hernia:**
- 9-15% association

### When to Investigate Further

**DSD (Disorders of Sex Development) Workup if:**
- Proximal hypospadias + cryptorchidism
- Bifid scrotum
- Ambiguous genitalia
- Micropenis

**Tests:**
- Karyotype
- Endocrine panel
- Imaging (US, genitography)`,
