        id: 'lipo-1',
        title: 'Liposuction Fundamentals',
        content: `## Liposuction Basics

### Evolution

**History:**
- 1974: Fischer (father of liposuction)
- 1977: Illouz (wet technique)
- 1987: Klein (tumescent)
- 1990s: Ultrasound, power, laser assisted

### Indications

**Good Candidates:**
- Localized fat deposits
- Good skin elasticity
- Stable weight
- Non-smoker
- Realistic expectations

**Body Areas:**
- Abdomen, flanks
- Thighs, buttocks
- Arms
- Neck/submental
- Gynecomastia

### Classification by Wetting

| Technique | Infiltrate:Aspirate | Description |
|-----------|---------------------|-------------|
| **Dry** | None | Historical, more bleeding |
| **Wet** | 200-300 mL total | Small volume |
| **Super-wet** | 1:1 | Most common |
| **Tumescent** | 3:1 | Pure local, high volume |

### Tumescent Technique

**Advantages:**
- Excellent anesthesia
- Minimal blood loss (<1% of aspirate)
- Hydrodissection
- No general anesthesia needed (for limited areas)

**Klein Solution:**
- 1000 mL NS or LR
- 500 mg lidocaine (50 mL of 1%)
- 1 mg epinephrine (1 mL of 1:1000)
- Optional: Sodium bicarbonate

**Maximum Dose:**
- Tumescent lidocaine: 35-55 mg/kg
- Peak levels at 8-12 hours

### Preoperative Marking

**Position:**
- Standing, natural posture
- Mark areas of excess
- Mark areas to avoid
- Identify asymmetries

**Zones:**
- Primary treatment zones
- Feathering zones (transitions)
- No-go zones (nerves, vessels)`,
