        id: 'gynecomastia-1',
        title: 'Etiology and Evaluation',
        content: `## Understanding Gynecomastia

### Definition

**Gynecomastia:**
- Benign proliferation of male breast glandular tissue
- ≥0.5 cm diameter subareolar tissue

**Pseudogynecomastia:**
- Lipomastia (fat only)
- No true glandular component

### Epidemiology

**Age Distribution:**
- Neonatal: 60-90% (maternal estrogens)
- Puberty: 50-60% (peak 13-14 years)
- Adult: Increases with age (>50 years)

### Etiology

**Physiologic (Most Common):**
- Neonatal
- Pubertal
- Senescent (age-related)

**Pathologic:**

| Category | Examples |
|----------|----------|
| **Drugs** | Spironolactone, cimetidine, ketoconazole, estrogens, marijuana, anabolic steroids |
| **Hormonal** | Hypogonadism, hyperthyroidism |
| **Tumors** | Testicular, adrenal, pituitary, HCG-secreting |
| **Systemic** | Liver cirrhosis, renal failure, malnutrition |
| **Idiopathic** | 25% of cases |

### Mechanism

**Hormonal Imbalance:**
- ↑ Estrogen or ↓ Androgen
- Ratio disturbance
- Estrogen stimulates breast tissue

### Clinical Evaluation

**History:**
- Duration and progression
- Pain or tenderness
- Medications and supplements
- Drug/alcohol use
- Symptoms of underlying disease

**Physical Exam:**
- Breast tissue vs fat (pinch test)
- Unilateral vs bilateral
- Tenderness
- Skin changes (rule out malignancy)
- Testicular exam
- Signs of liver/thyroid disease

### Workup

**When to Test:**
- Rapid onset
- Unilateral or asymmetric
- Signs of underlying disease
- Adult onset (not pubertal)

**Labs:**
- Testosterone (total, free)
- Estradiol
- LH, FSH
- Prolactin
- TSH
- Liver function tests
- β-hCG (if tumor suspected)`,
