        title: 'Wound Closure Principles',
        content: `## Optimal Wound Closure

### Principles

**Goals:**
1. Approximate wound edges
2. Minimize tension
3. Evert edges
4. Eliminate dead space
5. Preserve blood supply

### Tension Management

**Undermining:**
- Release tension
- Create advancement
- Preserve perforators

**Layered Closure:**
- Deep sutures take tension
- Skin closure tension-free
- Dead space eliminated

### Tissue Handling

**Atraumatic Technique:**
- Skin hooks (not forceps)
- Gentle manipulation
- Avoid crushing
- Minimize devitalization

**Hemostasis:**
- Prevent hematoma
- Avoid excessive cautery
- Drains if needed

### Suture Removal Timing

| Location | Removal Time |
|----------|--------------|
| Face | 5-7 days |
| Scalp | 7-10 days |
| Trunk | 10-14 days |
| Extremities | 10-14 days |
| Joints | 14 days |
| Palms/soles | 14-21 days |

**Early Removal:**
- Prevents suture marks
- May need steri-strips

### Alternative Closures

**Skin Adhesive (Dermabond):**
- Cyanoacrylate
- Low-tension wounds
- Waterproof

**Staples:**
- Fast application
- Scalp, trunk
- Remove 7-14 days

**Steri-Strips:**
- Supplement closure
- Low tension
- After suture removal

### Special Considerations

**Contaminated Wounds:**
- Delayed primary closure
- Pack open, close in 3-5 days
- Reduces infection

**High-Tension Areas:**
- Layered closure essential
- May need retention sutures
- Slow healing

**Irradiated Tissue:**
- Poor healing
- Consider flap coverage
- Extra suture time`,
