        content: `## Syndromic Forms

### FGFR-Related Syndromes

**Common Mutations:**
- FGFR1, FGFR2, FGFR3
- Autosomal dominant
- Variable expressivity

### Crouzon Syndrome

**Features:**
- Bilateral coronal synostosis (most common)
- Multiple sutures may fuse
- Midface hypoplasia
- Proptosis
- Normal hands and feet
- Normal intelligence

**FGFR2 mutation**

### Apert Syndrome

**Features:**
- Bilateral coronal synostosis
- Severe midface hypoplasia
- Syndactyly of hands and feet (mitten hands)
- Turricephaly
- Intellectual disability (variable)

**FGFR2 mutation (Ser252Trp or Pro253Arg)**

### Pfeiffer Syndrome

**Features:**
- Craniosynostosis
- Broad thumbs and great toes
- Brachydactyly
- Variable severity

**Types:**
| Type | Features |
|------|----------|
| I | Mild, good prognosis |
| II | Cloverleaf skull, severe |
| III | Severe without cloverleaf |

**FGFR1 or FGFR2 mutation**

### Saethre-Chotzen Syndrome

**Features:**
- Coronal synostosis
- Ptosis
- Low-set hairline
- Brachydactyly
- Syndactyly 2-3 fingers

**TWIST1 mutation**

### Muenke Syndrome

**Features:**
- Coronal synostosis (uni or bilateral)
- May have normal appearance
- Hearing loss
- Brachydactyly

**FGFR3 mutation (Pro250Arg)**

### Cloverleaf Skull (Kleeblattschädel)

**Features:**
- Multiple suture involvement
- Trilobular skull shape
- Severe proptosis
- Very high ICP risk

**Associated with:**
- Pfeiffer Type II
- Crouzon (rare)
- Thanatophoric dysplasia`,
