        id: 'maxfx-2',
        title: 'Mandible Fractures',
        content: `## Mandible Fracture Management

### Anatomy

**Weak Points:**
- Angle (third molar)
- Condylar neck
- Parasymphysis (mental foramen)

**Muscle Forces:**
- Masseter, temporalis: Elevate
- Digastric, mylohyoid: Depress
- Pterygoids: Lateral deviation

### Classification

**By Location:**
| Region | Percentage |
|--------|------------|
| Condyle | 30-35% |
| Angle | 25-30% |
| Symphysis/Parasymphysis | 20-25% |
| Body | 15-20% |
| Ramus | 3-5% |
| Coronoid | 1-2% |

**Favorable vs Unfavorable:**
- Favorable: Muscle forces reduce fracture
- Unfavorable: Muscle forces distract fracture

### Treatment Principles

**Goals:**
- Restore occlusion
- Bone healing
- Function (mastication, mouth opening)

**Options:**
- Closed reduction (MMF)
- Open reduction internal fixation (ORIF)
- External fixation (rarely)

### Closed Reduction (MMF)

**Indications:**
- Stable fractures
- Condylar fractures (many)
- Children (to preserve tooth buds)

**Technique:**
- Arch bars or IMF screws
- Maxillomandibular fixation
- 4-6 weeks

### Open Reduction Internal Fixation

**Indications:**
- Displaced fractures
- Unfavorable fractures
- Patient compliance concerns
- Gunshot wounds

**Champy's Lines:**
- Ideal plate placement
- Along tension zones
- Superior border: Tension band

**Plate Options:**
| Plate | Use |
|-------|-----|
| Miniplates | Most common |
| Reconstruction plates | Comminuted, defects |
| Lag screws | Specific patterns |

### Condylar Fractures

**Treatment Controversy:**
- Many treated closed
- Open for: Dislocation, height loss, lateral displacement

**Open Indications:**
- Dislocation into middle fossa
- Lateral extracapsular displacement
- Foreign body
- Bilateral with anterior open bite`,
