        id: 'mast-4',
        title: 'Complications',
        content: `## Mastopexy Complications

### Wound Complications

**T-Junction Dehiscence:**
- Most common site
- Usually minor
- Local wound care
- Avoid tight closure

**Skin Necrosis:**
- Risk factors: Smoking, tension
- Prevention: Smoking cessation
- Treatment: Debridement, healing

### NAC Complications

**Partial Necrosis:**
- More common than total
- Due to pedicle compromise
- Treatment: Wound care, reconstruction

**Total NAC Loss:**
- Rare with proper pedicle
- Risk: Smoking, diabetes
- Reconstruction: Graft, flap, tattoo

**Sensation Loss:**
- Often temporary
- Permanent in 5-10%
- Warn patients preoperatively

### Shape Issues

**Bottoming Out:**
- NAC rides high
- Lower pole stretches
- Treatment: Revision, internal sutures

**Recurrent Ptosis:**
- Common over time
- Skin laxity
- May need revision

**Asymmetry:**
- Often pre-existing
- May worsen or improve
- Document preoperatively

### Scar Issues

**Hypertrophic Scars:**
- Common in breast
- Treatment: Silicone, steroids
- May need revision

**Widened Scars:**
- Periareolar especially
- Treatment: Revision, mesh

### Augmentation-Mastopexy

**Higher Complication Rate:**
- Double bubble
- Implant malposition
- Bottoming out
- Consider staging

### Results and Satisfaction

**Duration:**
- Initial result: 6-12 weeks
- Settling: 3-6 months
- Long-term: Gravity recurs

**Satisfaction:**
- Generally high
- Scars are trade-off
- Realistic expectations`,
