        id: 'lesion-2',
        title: 'Basal Cell Carcinoma',
        content: `## Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC)

### Epidemiology

**Most Common Cancer in Humans**
- 4-5 million cases/year in US
- Sun exposure primary risk
- Rarely metastasizes (<0.1%)
- Locally destructive

### Risk Factors

- UV exposure (cumulative)
- Fair skin (Fitzpatrick I-II)
- Immunosuppression
- Arsenic exposure
- Previous radiation
- Genetic: Gorlin syndrome (PTCH1)

### Clinical Types

| Type | Features | Behavior |
|------|----------|----------|
| **Nodular** | Pearly, telangiectasia, rolled border | Most common (60-80%) |
| **Superficial** | Scaly, erythematous, flat | Trunk, multiple |
| **Morpheaform** | Scar-like, ill-defined | Most aggressive |
| **Pigmented** | Dark color | May mimic melanoma |

### Diagnosis

**Biopsy:**
- Shave (superficial)
- Punch (most common)
- Excisional (small lesions)

**Dermoscopy:**
- Arborizing vessels
- Blue-gray ovoid nests
- Ulceration

### Treatment Options

**Surgical:**
| Method | Indication |
|--------|------------|
| Excision | Standard treatment |
| Mohs | High-risk, face, recurrent |
| Curettage & Electrodesiccation | Low-risk, superficial |

**Non-Surgical:**
- Topical (imiquimod, 5-FU): Superficial only
- Radiation: Not surgical candidate
- Targeted therapy (vismodegib): Advanced/metastatic

### Excision Margins

| Risk Level | Margin |
|------------|--------|
| Low-risk, <2cm | 4mm |
| High-risk or >2cm | 5-10mm or Mohs |

### High-Risk Features

- Size >2cm
- Location: H-zone (central face)
- Morpheaform subtype
- Perineural invasion
- Recurrent
- Immunosuppression`,
