        content: `## Polydactyly

### Classification

**By Location:**
| Type | Location | Incidence |
|------|----------|-----------|
| Preaxial | Thumb (radial) | Common in Caucasians |
| Central | Middle digits | Rare |
| Postaxial | Small finger (ulnar) | Common in African Americans |

### Thumb Duplication (Preaxial)

**Wassel Classification:**
| Type | Description | Most Common |
|------|-------------|-------------|
| I | Bifid distal phalanx | |
| II | Duplicated distal phalanx | |
| III | Bifid proximal phalanx | |
| **IV** | **Duplicated proximal phalanx** | **Most common (40%)** |
| V | Bifid metacarpal | |
| VI | Duplicated metacarpal | |
| VII | Triphalangeal | |

### Treatment - Thumb Duplication

**Goals:**
- Single, functional thumb
- Adequate stability
- Good appearance

**Technique:**
- Usually ablate smaller/more radial
- Reconstruct collateral ligaments
- May need osteotomies
- Preserve best elements of each

### Postaxial Polydactyly

**Type A:** Well-formed digit
- Requires formal excision

**Type B:** Rudimentary/pedunculated
- Suture ligation in nursery
- Or formal excision

## Thumb Hypoplasia

### Blauth Classification

| Grade | Features | Treatment |
|-------|----------|-----------|
| **I** | Minor hypoplasia | Usually none |
| **II** | Narrow first web, thenar hypoplasia | Opposition transfer |
| **IIIA** | Unstable MCP, intrinsic deficiency | Reconstruction |
| **IIIB** | CMC abnormal, extrinsic abnormal | Pollicization |
| **IV** | Pouce flottant (floating thumb) | Pollicization |
| **V** | Absent thumb | Pollicization |

### Pollicization

**Indication:**
- Blauth IIIB, IV, V
- Non-reconstructible thumb

**Technique:**
- Index finger transposed to thumb position
- Shortened
- Rotated 160°
- Intrinsics become thenar muscles

**Timing:**
- 6-12 months typically
- Before grasp patterns established`,
