        id: 'nail-4',
        title: 'Replantation and Complex Injuries',
        content: `## Complex Fingertip Management

### Composite Graft

**Definition:**
- Amputated part as graft (no vascular anastomosis)
- Survives by imbibition

**Best Success:**
- Children
- Small parts (<1 cm)
- Clean amputations

**Technique:**
- Debride minimally
- Replace anatomically
- Dress with occlusive dressing

### Replantation

**Indications (Fingertip):**
- Thumb (always attempt)
- Children (excellent tolerance)
- Multiple digits
- Sharp amputations

**Relative Contraindications:**
- Single digit (other than thumb)
- Crush injury
- Avulsion
- Severely contaminated

### Venous Congestion

**Problem:**
- Arterial inflow > venous outflow
- Common in distal replants

**Treatment:**
- Nail removal (bleeding)
- Heparin-soaked pledgets
- Medicinal leeches
- Fish-mouth incisions

### Leeches

**Indications:**
- Venous congestion
- Failed venous anastomosis

**Protocol:**
- Apply to congested area
- Replace when drops off
- Monitor hemoglobin
- Prophylactic antibiotics (Aeromonas)

### Hook Nail Deformity

**Cause:**
- Loss of bone support for nail
- Nail curves over tip

**Prevention:**
- Maintain bone length
- Skin graft to support nail
- Ensure nail bed support

**Treatment:**
- Bone graft
- Nail bed advancement
- Revision

### Outcomes

**Prognosis Factors:**
- Age (children better)
- Mechanism (sharp better)
- Level (more distal better)
- Contamination
- Ischemia time

### Rehabilitation

**Post-operative:**
- Early motion when stable
- Desensitization for hypersensitivity
- Sensory re-education
- Therapy involvement

**Recovery Timeline:**
- Initial healing: 3-6 weeks
- Sensory return: 3-6 months
- Nail regrowth: 4-6 months`,
