        content: `## Flexor Tendon Complications

### Rupture

**Incidence:** 3-5%

**Causes:**
- Premature activity
- Weak repair
- Poor technique

**Prevention:**
- Strong repair (≥4 strand)
- Patient compliance
- Proper rehabilitation

**Treatment:**
- Re-repair if early
- Grafting if late

### Adhesions

**Most Common Complication**

**Risk Factors:**
- Immobilization
- Crush injury
- Multiple repairs
- Sheath damage

**Prevention:**
- Early motion
- Atraumatic technique
- Minimize sheath damage

**Treatment:**
- Therapy first
- Tenolysis if plateau

### Bowstringing

**Cause:**
- Pulley loss (A2 or A4)

**Prevention:**
- Preserve A2 and A4
- Vent pulleys strategically

**Treatment:**
- Pulley reconstruction
- Tendon graft material

### Flexion Contracture

**Cause:**
- Prolonged immobilization
- Joint contracture

**Prevention:**
- IP extension in splint
- Early motion

**Treatment:**
- Therapy/splinting
- Capsulotomy if severe

### Special Situations

**Pediatric:**
- Often zone I avulsions
- May heal without repair (zone I)
- Shorter rehabilitation

**Jersey Finger (FDP Avulsion):**

| Type | Level of Retraction |
|------|---------------------|
| I | Palm (no vinculum) |
| II | PIP level |
| III | A4 pulley (with bone) |

**Treatment Timing:**
- Type I: Urgent (7 days - vinculum lost)
- Type II: 2-3 weeks
- Type III: Several weeks (bone blocks)

### Tendon Grafting

**Indications:**
- Failed primary repair
- Delayed presentation
- Segmental loss

**Donor:**
- Palmaris longus (same arm)
- Plantaris
- Long toe extensors

**Stages:**
- One-stage: Good sheath, bed
- Two-stage: Rod first, graft second`,
