        title: 'Complications and Special Topics',
        content: `## Complications and Advanced Topics

### Complications

**Poor Growth:**
- Causes: Desiccation, trauma, poor technique
- Prevention: Keep grafts moist, atraumatic handling
- May need revision

**Unnatural Appearance:**
- Wrong direction/angle
- Pluggy look (old technique)
- Inappropriate hairline
- Prevention: Single FUs at hairline, proper angles

**Donor Area Issues:**
- FUT: Wide/visible scar
- FUE: Overharvest, moth-eaten
- Prevention: Proper closure, conservative harvest

**Infection:**
- Rare but possible
- Antibiotics, wound care

**Cobblestoning:**
- Grafts sit above skin level
- Usually resolves
- Steroid injection if persistent

### Special Situations

**Female Pattern:**
- Usually FUT preferred
- Donor often good
- Rule out other causes
- Different pattern than males

**Eyebrow Transplant:**
- Fine single-hair FUs
- Specific angle (horizontal)
- High maintenance (trimming)

**Beard/Body Hair:**
- BHT (Body Hair Transplant)
- Finer, different growth cycle
- Supplement scalp donor

### PRP (Platelet-Rich Plasma)

**Adjunct Therapy:**
- Concentrated growth factors
- May improve graft survival
- May stimulate native hair

**Technique:**
- Blood draw
- Centrifuge to concentrate platelets
- Inject into scalp

### Future Directions

**Hair Cloning:**
- Follicle regeneration
- Not yet clinically available
- Research ongoing

**Stem Cell Therapy:**
- Dermal papilla cells
- Hair follicle neogenesis
- Future potential

### Patient Selection

**Good Candidates:**
- Realistic expectations
- Adequate donor density
- Stable pattern or on medical therapy
- Understand limitations

**Poor Candidates:**
- Unrealistic expectations
- Poor donor area
- Diffuse unpatterned alopecia
- Medical causes of hair loss`,
