        id: 'ortho-3',
        title: 'Mandibular Osteotomies',
        content: `## Mandibular Surgery

### Bilateral Sagittal Split Osteotomy (BSSO)

**Most Common Mandibular Osteotomy**

**Indications:**
- Mandibular prognathism (setback)
- Mandibular retrognathia (advancement)
- Asymmetry correction
- Class II or III correction

**Technique:**
- Intraoral approach
- Medial horizontal cut (above lingula)
- Sagittal split through ramus
- Buccal vertical cut
- Split mandible
- Move distal segment
- Rigid fixation

**Fixation:**
- Bicortical screws (3 per side)
- Plate and screw
- Hybrid techniques

**Advantages:**
- Large contact surface
- Good healing
- Versatile movement

### Intraoral Vertical Ramus Osteotomy (IVRO)

**Alternative for Setback:**
- Vertical cut through ramus
- Used for mandibular setback
- Less nerve risk
- Requires IMF (6 weeks)

### Genioplasty

**Chin Osteotomy:**

**Indications:**
- Chin deficiency
- Chin excess
- Asymmetry

**Movements:**
- Advancement
- Setback
- Lengthening (vertical increase)
- Shortening (vertical decrease)
- Lateral shift

**Technique:**
- Intraoral approach
- Oscillating saw below tooth roots
- Above mental nerves
- Plate fixation

### Inferior Alveolar Nerve

**Runs Through Mandible:**
- At risk in BSSO
- Temporary numbness common
- Permanent injury: 2-5%

**Protection:**
- Careful splitting
- Direct visualization
- Protect during fixation

### Distraction Osteogenesis

**Indications:**
- Large advancements
- Hypoplastic mandible (hemifacial microsomia)
- Avoids nerve stretching

**Technique:**
- Osteotomy
- Apply distractor
- Latency period (5-7 days)
- Distraction (1 mm/day)
- Consolidation (6-8 weeks)`,
