        title: 'Genital Surgery',
        content: `## Genital Reconstruction

### Vaginoplasty (MTF)

**Techniques:**
- Penile inversion (most common)
- Intestinal (sigmoid, ileum)
- Peritoneal pull-through

**Penile Inversion:**
- Penile/scrotal skin for vaginal lining
- Clitoris from glans
- Labia from scrotal skin
- Urethral shortening

**Depth:**
- Variable (4-6+ inches)
- May need skin graft if limited

**Dilation:**
- Critical for maintaining depth
- Lifelong requirement
- Especially first year

**Complications:**
- Stenosis (if inadequate dilation)
- Prolapse
- Rectovaginal fistula
- Need for revision

### Phalloplasty (FTM)

**Flap Options:**
| Flap | Pros | Cons |
|------|------|------|
| Radial forearm | Sensate, reliable | Donor scar |
| ALT | Less visible scar | Bulkier |
| Abdominal | No arm scar | Multiple stages |

**Components:**
- Phallus creation (flap)
- Urethroplasty (pee standing)
- Scrotoplasty
- Penile prosthesis (optional)

**Stages:**
- Often 2-3+ surgeries
- Urethroplasty complication rate high
- Prosthesis last

### Metoidioplasty (FTM)

**Alternative to Phalloplasty:**
- Uses testosterone-enlarged clitoris
- Creates small phallus
- Less morbidity
- Can void standing (sometimes)
- No erection device needed

**Limitations:**
- Small size (usually <5 cm)
- May not allow penetration

### Complications

**Urethral:**
- Fistula (most common)
- Stricture
- Often need revision

**General:**
- Wound complications
- Flap loss (rare)
- Functional issues`,
