        id: 'uet-3',
        title: 'Soft Tissue Injuries',
        content: `## Soft Tissue Coverage

### Assessment

**Coverage Needs:**
- Exposed bone
- Exposed tendon
- Exposed hardware
- Exposed neurovascular

### Reconstructive Ladder

**Options (Simplest to Complex):**
1. Primary closure
2. Skin grafting
3. Local flaps
4. Regional flaps
5. Free tissue transfer

### Local Flaps - Hand

**Digital Flaps:**
| Flap | Use |
|------|-----|
| V-Y advancement | Fingertip |
| Cross-finger | Finger coverage |
| Thenar | Finger, thumb tip |
| Moberg | Thumb tip |
| First dorsal metacarpal | Thumb |

**Cross-Finger Flap:**
- From adjacent finger dorsum
- Two stages (division at 2-3 weeks)
- Covers volar fingertip/finger

### Regional Flaps

**Groin Flap:**
- Based on SCIA
- Large coverage
- Pedicled: Hand attached 3 weeks
- Can be transferred as free flap

**Posterior Interosseous Artery (PIA) Flap:**
- Pedicled from forearm
- Covers dorsal hand

**Radial Forearm Flap:**
- Free or pedicled (retrograde)
- Fasciocutaneous
- Thin, pliable

### Free Tissue Transfer

**Common Free Flaps:**
| Flap | Use |
|------|-----|
| ALT | Large defects |
| Lateral arm | Moderate defects |
| Temporoparietal fascia | Thin coverage |
| Serratus | Thin, pliable |
| Toe pulp | Sensate fingertip |

### Timing

**Emergent Coverage:**
- Exposed vital structures
- Heavily contaminated wounds

**Early (Within Days):**
- Better outcomes
- Reduced infection`,
