        title: 'Assessment and Principles',
        content: `## Hand Infections Overview

### Significance

**Why Important:**
- Enclosed compartments
- Vital structures at risk
- Rapid spread
- Functional impairment
- Potential amputation

### Common Organisms

| Organism | Setting |
|----------|---------|
| Staph aureus | Most common overall |
| MRSA | Community, healthcare |
| Streptococcus | Rapidly spreading |
| Eikenella | Human bite |
| Pasteurella | Cat/dog bite |
| Polymicrobial | Diabetic, IV drug use |

### History

**Key Questions:**
- Time of onset
- Mechanism (trauma, bite)
- Occupation
- Animal exposure
- Diabetes/immunocompromise
- Previous infections
- Recent antibiotics

### Physical Examination

**Cardinal Signs:**
- Rubor (redness)
- Calor (warmth)
- Tumor (swelling)
- Dolor (pain)
- Loss of function

**Specific Signs:**
| Sign | Infection |
|------|-----------|
| Fusiform swelling | Flexor tenosynovitis |
| Collar button | Web space abscess |
| Thenar swelling | Thenar space infection |

### Kanavel Signs

**Flexor Tenosynovitis:**
1. Fusiform (uniform) finger swelling
2. Finger held in flexion
3. Tenderness over flexor sheath
4. Pain with passive extension

### Laboratory

**Basic Work-up:**
- CBC (WBC, differential)
- CRP, ESR
- Blood cultures (if febrile)
- Wound culture

### Imaging

**X-ray:**
- Foreign body
- Osteomyelitis
- Gas in tissues

**MRI/Ultrasound:**
- Deep space infections
- Abscess localization`,
