        content: `## Superficial Hand Infections

### Paronychia

**Definition:**
- Infection of nail fold
- Lateral or proximal

**Acute:**
- Usually Staph aureus
- Hangnail or manicure
- Purulent collection

**Treatment:**
- Early: Warm soaks, antibiotics
- Abscess: I&D (lift nail fold)
- May need partial nail removal

**Chronic:**
- Candida common
- Wet exposure (dishwashers)
- Marsupialisation of nail fold

### Felon

**Definition:**
- Closed space infection
- Fingertip pulp
- Multiple septae involved

**Presentation:**
- Severe throbbing pain
- Tense, swollen fingertip
- May progress to osteomyelitis

**Treatment:**
- I&D required
- Incision options:
  - Lateral (preferred)
  - Fishmouth (avoid)
  - High lateral
- Divide all septae
- Pack wound open

### Herpetic Whitlow

**Viral Infection:**
- HSV-1 or HSV-2
- Healthcare workers at risk
- Vesicles with clear fluid

**Diagnosis:**
- Clinical appearance
- Tzanck smear
- PCR

**Treatment:**
- Self-limiting (2-3 weeks)
- Antiviral (acyclovir) if early
- DO NOT I&D (spreads virus)

### Cellulitis

**Definition:**
- Spreading skin/subcutaneous infection
- No abscess collection

**Treatment:**
- IV or oral antibiotics
- Elevation
- Mark borders
- Splint for comfort

**When to Suspect Deeper:**
- Not improving on antibiotics
- Fluctuance develops
- Worsening despite treatment`,
