        title: 'Carpal Tunnel Syndrome',
        content: `## Carpal Tunnel Syndrome

### Definition

**Median Nerve Compression:**
- At wrist (carpal tunnel)
- Most common compression neuropathy
- More common in females

### Anatomy

**Carpal Tunnel:**
- Floor: Carpal bones
- Roof: Transverse carpal ligament
- Contents: Median nerve + 9 flexor tendons

### Risk Factors

| Factor | Association |
|--------|-------------|
| Pregnancy | Fluid retention |
| Diabetes | Neuropathy |
| Hypothyroidism | Tissue changes |
| Rheumatoid arthritis | Tenosynovitis |
| Obesity | Increased pressure |
| Repetitive motion | Occupational |

### Symptoms

**Classic Presentation:**
- Numbness in median distribution (thumb, index, middle, radial ring)
- Night symptoms (wake up shaking hand)
- Thenar weakness (late)
- Drop objects

**Provocative Tests:**
| Test | Maneuver | Positive |
|------|----------|----------|
| Phalen | Wrist flexed 60 sec | Paresthesias |
| Tinel | Tap over carpal tunnel | Tingling |
| Durkan | Direct compression | Paresthesias |

### Electrodiagnostic Studies

**NCS/EMG:**
- Sensory latency prolonged first
- Motor latency prolonged
- Thenar EMG: Denervation (late)

**Severity:**
- Mild: Sensory only
- Moderate: Sensory + motor
- Severe: Denervation

### Non-operative Treatment

**Conservative:**
- Night splint (neutral wrist)
- Activity modification
- NSAIDs
- Corticosteroid injection

**Response:**
- Mild cases may respond
- Steroid injection: Diagnostic and therapeutic
- Many ultimately need surgery

### Surgical Treatment

**Open Release:**
- Standard technique
- Longitudinal incision
- Divide transverse carpal ligament

**Endoscopic Release:**
- One or two portal
- Faster recovery
- Similar outcomes`,
