        id: 'perf-1',
        title: 'Perforator Flap Concepts',
        content: `## Perforator Flap Fundamentals

### Definition

**Perforator Flap:**
- Skin and subcutaneous tissue
- Supplied by isolated perforating vessel
- Muscle NOT included
- Named by source vessel

### Historical Evolution

**Progression:**
- Muscle flaps (whole muscle)
- Musculocutaneous flaps
- Fasciocutaneous flaps
- Perforator flaps (current)

### Advantages

**Donor Site:**
- Muscle preserved (function)
- Less morbidity
- Faster recovery
- Smaller scar

**Recipient:**
- Thin, pliable flap
- Can contour
- Long pedicle possible

### Nomenclature

**Name Format:**
- Source artery + perforator
- Example: DIEP = Deep Inferior Epigastric Perforator

**Common Perforator Flaps:**
| Flap | Source Vessel |
|------|---------------|
| DIEP | Deep inferior epigastric a. |
| SGAP | Superior gluteal a. |
| IGAP | Inferior gluteal a. |
| ALT | Descending branch LCFA |
| TDAP | Thoracodorsal a. |
| PAP | Profunda a. |

### Angiosome Concept

**Definition:**
- 3D territory of a source artery
- Skin and underlying tissue
- Connected to adjacent angiosomes

**Significance:**
- Safe flap design
- Capture territory with perforators

### Perforator Selection

**Ideal Perforator:**
- Large diameter
- Long intramuscular course
- Central location
- Palpable pulse

**Imaging:**
- CTA (most common)
- MRA
- Handheld doppler`,
