        content: `## Thigh Perforator Flaps

### ALT (Anterolateral Thigh)

**Workhorse Free Flap:**

**Anatomy:**
- Descending branch LCFA
- Septocutaneous or musculocutaneous perforators
- Through vastus lateralis or septum

**Advantages:**
- Large flap possible
- Long pedicle
- Thin or thick
- Sensate option
- Primary closure possible

**Uses:**
- Head and neck reconstruction
- Extremity coverage
- Oral cavity
- Pharyngoesophageal

**Chimeric Options:**
- Include vastus lateralis
- Include tensor fascia lata

### PAP (Profunda Artery Perforator)

**Emerging Alternative:**

**Anatomy:**
- Profunda femoris perforators
- Posterior thigh
- Through adductor magnus

**Advantages:**
- Concealed scar
- Soft tissue quality
- Good for breast

**Disadvantages:**
- Shorter pedicle
- Patient positioning

### TFL (Tensor Fascia Lata)

**Can be harvested as perforator:**
- LCFA perforators
- Reduces donor morbidity

### Gracilis Perforator

**Anatomy:**
- Medial circumflex femoral perforators
- Medial thigh

**Uses:**
- TMG (transverse myocutaneous gracilis) for breast
- Can be true perforator flap

### Considerations

**Thigh Flaps:**
| Flap | Pedicle Length | Thickness |
|------|---------------|-----------|
| ALT | Long (8-15 cm) | Variable |
| PAP | Shorter (6-8 cm) | Moderate |
| Gracilis | Short | Thin |

**Primary Closure:**
- ALT: Width <8-10 cm
- PAP: Most close primarily
- Gracilis: Closes easily`,
