        title: 'Lymphedema and Surveillance',
        content: `## Post-Treatment Considerations

### Lymphedema After LND

**Risk:**
- Groin dissection: 30-40%
- Axillary dissection: 20-30%
- Neck: Less common

**Prevention:**
- Sartorius/TFL transposition (groin)
- Compression
- Physical therapy
- Lymph node transfer (complex)

### Surveillance

**Follow-up Schedule:**
| Stage | Frequency |
|-------|-----------|
| 0-II | Q3-6 months × 5 years |
| III-IV | Q3 months × 3 years |

**Components:**
- Skin exam (entire body)
- Lymph node palpation
- Scar assessment
- Imaging as indicated

### Recurrence Detection

**Types:**
- Local (at primary site)
- In-transit (between primary and nodes)
- Regional (lymph nodes)
- Distant (metastatic)

**Signs:**
- New nodule at or near scar
- Satellite lesions
- Lymphadenopathy
- Systemic symptoms

### Reconstruction Considerations

**Allowing Surveillance:**
- Skin grafts easier to monitor
- Flaps may hide recurrence
- Document scar appearance
- Patient education for self-exam

### Managing Recurrence

**Local Recurrence:**
- Re-excision
- May need wider margins
- Consider SLNB if not done

**In-Transit:**
- Isolated limb perfusion/infusion
- Systemic therapy
- T-VEC (injectable)
- Surgical excision

### Multidisciplinary Care

**Team Approach:**
- Surgical oncology
- Medical oncology
- Plastic surgery
- Dermatology
- Radiation oncology (selected cases)`,
