        content: `## Location-Based Approach

### Earlobe Keloids

**Most Common Keloid:**
- Usually from piercing
- Relatively good prognosis

**Treatment:**
- Surgical excision
- Core excision technique
- Immediate steroid injection
- Pressure earring post-op

**Recurrence:** 10-20% with combined approach

### Sternal Keloids

**Most Challenging:**
- High tension area
- Very high recurrence
- Difficult to pressure

**Treatment:**
- Intralesional excision
- Radiation therapy essential
- Long-term steroid injections
- Z-plasty to redirect tension

### Shoulder/Back

**Similar to Sternum:**
- High tension
- Prone to recurrence

**Management:**
- Conservative first
- Surgery + radiation if needed
- Prolonged pressure therapy

### Prevention Strategies

**High-Risk Patients:**
- Avoid elective procedures
- Minimal tension closure
- Prophylactic steroid injection
- Silicone from early healing

**Surgical Technique:**
- Tension-free closure
- Avoid midline chest incisions
- Subcuticular closure
- Early silicone therapy

### Emerging Therapies

**Botulinum Toxin:**
- Reduces wound tension
- May reduce recurrence
- Adjunct to excision

**Imiquimod:**
- Immune modulator
- Post-excision application
- Variable results

**Targeted Therapies:**
- TGF-β inhibitors
- Anti-CTGF antibodies
- Future potential

### Patient Counseling

**Set Expectations:**
- High recurrence risk
- Multiple treatments likely
- Long-term management
- No guaranteed cure`,
