        title: 'Arterial vs Venous Compromise',
        content: `# Recognizing Flap Compromise

## Arterial Insufficiency

### Signs
| Finding | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Color | Pale, white, waxy |
| Cap refill | Slow >3 seconds or absent |
| Turgor | Decreased, flaccid |
| Temperature | Cool to cold |
| Pin prick | No bleeding |
| Doppler | Absent or weak signal |

### Causes
- Arterial thrombus
- Kinking of pedicle
- External compression
- Vasospasm
- Systemic hypotension

## Venous Congestion

### Signs
| Finding | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Color | Dusky, blue, purple |
| Cap refill | Rapid <1 second or flash |
| Turgor | Turgid, swollen |
| Temperature | Initially warm, then cool |
| Pin prick | Rapid dark bleeding |
| Doppler | May still have arterial signal |

### Causes
- Venous thrombus
- Kinking/compression of vein
- Tight closure/dressing
- Dependent positioning
- Hematoma compression

## Comparison Table

| Feature | Arterial | Venous |
|---------|----------|--------|
| Color | Pale/white | Dusky/purple |
| Cap refill | Slow/absent | Fast/flash |
| Turgor | Flaccid | Turgid |
| Temperature | Cold | Initially warm |
| Pin prick | No bleeding | Dark, rapid bleeding |

## Response Protocol

### Immediate Actions
1. Notify surgeon IMMEDIATELY
2. Remove any dressings
3. Check positioning
4. Release any tight sutures if instructed
5. Optimize patient conditions
6. Prepare for possible return to OR`,
