        title: 'Monitoring Devices and Techniques',
        content: `# Flap Monitoring Methods

## Clinical Monitoring (Gold Standard)

### Benefits
- No equipment needed
- Direct tissue assessment
- Reliable when done properly

### Limitations
- Subjective
- Observer variability
- Buried flaps not assessable

## Handheld Doppler

### Technique
- Mark vessel location intraop
- Check arterial signal regularly
- Compare to baseline

### Interpretation
| Finding | Meaning |
|---------|---------|
| Strong triphasic | Normal arterial |
| Weak/monophasic | Possible compromise |
| Absent | Arterial problem |
| Venous hum | May mask issues |

## Implantable Doppler

### Advantages
- Continuous monitoring
- Buried flap assessment
- Objective alarm system
- Earlier detection

### Considerations
- Requires intraop placement
- Cost of probes
- False alarms possible

## Other Technologies

### Color Duplex
- Non-invasive
- Visualizes flow
- Operator dependent

### Near-Infrared Spectroscopy
- Measures tissue oxygenation
- Continuous monitoring
- Learning curve

### Tissue Oximetry
- Measures StO2
- Trend monitoring
- May detect changes early

## Monitoring Environment

### Optimize Conditions
| Factor | Goal |
|--------|------|
| Temperature | Room warm (75-80°F) |
| Lighting | Adequate to assess color |
| Positioning | Avoid pedicle compression |
| Dressings | Allow flap visualization |
| Blood pressure | Avoid hypotension |
| Hydration | Euvolemic |`,
