        id: 'scar-4',
        title: 'Keloid and Complex Scar Management',
        content: `# Complex Scar Management

## Keloid Treatment Protocol

### First-Line
1. Intralesional steroids
2. Silicone therapy
3. Compression (if feasible)

### Refractory Keloids
| Treatment | Details |
|-----------|---------|
| Excision + steroids | Inject at time of excision |
| Excision + radiation | XRT within 24-48 hours |
| Cryotherapy | Serial treatments |
| 5-FU + steroids | Combined injection |

### Excision Principles
- Intralesional excision (not complete)
- Immediate steroid injection
- Consider radiation for recurrent
- Postop silicone and pressure

### Radiation Therapy
- External beam or brachytherapy
- Start within 24-48 hours of excision
- Reduces recurrence 50-90%
- Consider risk in young patients

## Surgical Revision

### Indications
- Wide or spread scar
- Contracture causing function loss
- Misaligned scar
- Trap door deformity
- Failed conservative treatment

### Techniques
| Problem | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| Wide scar | Re-excision with layered closure |
| Linear contracture | Z-plasty, W-plasty |
| Trap door | Defatting, revision |
| Depressed scar | Fat grafting, filler |

### Timing
- Wait 12-18 months if possible
- Earlier if functional impairment
- Document maturation

## Patient Counseling

### Key Messages
- Scars are permanent
- Improvement, not perfection
- Treatment takes time
- Multiple modalities often needed
- Recurrence possible with keloids`,
