        id: 'outpt-3',
        title: 'Procedure Limits and Combination Safety',
        content: `# Procedure Safety Limits

## Duration Limits

### General Guidelines
| Duration | Consideration |
|----------|---------------|
| <3 hours | Standard outpatient |
| 3-6 hours | Careful patient selection |
| >6 hours | Consider inpatient |

### Factors Affecting Duration Limits
- Patient health status
- Complexity of procedure
- Anesthesia type
- Surgeon experience
- Facility capabilities

## Combined Procedures

### Risk Considerations
| Factor | Impact |
|--------|--------|
| Total time | Cumulative anesthesia risk |
| Blood loss | Sum of all procedures |
| VTE risk | Increases with duration |
| Recovery | More complex |

### Guidelines for Combining
- Total time should not exceed facility limits
- Consider staging for high-risk patients
- VTE prophylaxis more aggressive
- Blood loss planning

## Liposuction Safety

### Volume Limits
| Type | Limit |
|------|-------|
| Superwet/tumescent | 5 L total aspirate |
| Large volume (>5L) | Consider inpatient |
| With other procedures | Reduce limits |

### Tumescent Dosing
- Max lidocaine: 35-55 mg/kg
- Calculate total dose carefully
- Monitor for LAST

## Abdominoplasty Considerations

### Outpatient Criteria
- Healthy patient (ASA I-II)
- BMI <35 preferred
- No combined major procedures
- Adequate home support

### Combined with Liposuction
- Limit total volume
- Higher complication risk
- Consider staging
- VTE prophylaxis essential

## Fat Transfer Safety

### BBL Special Concerns
- Highest mortality rate in aesthetics
- Fat embolism risk
- Subcutaneous injection ONLY
- Monitor postoperatively`,
