        id: 'outpt-4',
        title: 'Emergency Management in Office Setting',
        content: `# Office Emergency Management

## Emergency Response Plan

### Essential Components
1. Written emergency protocols
2. Assigned roles and responsibilities
3. Regular drills (quarterly minimum)
4. Emergency contact numbers posted
5. EMS notification procedure
6. Transfer protocol

## Common Emergencies

### Cardiac Arrest
1. Call for help / Activate EMS
2. Begin CPR
3. Get AED/defibrillator
4. Follow ACLS algorithm
5. Continue until EMS arrives

### Anaphylaxis
1. Stop suspected trigger
2. Epinephrine IM (0.3-0.5mg)
3. Airway management
4. IV fluids
5. Repeat epinephrine q5 min
6. Call EMS

### Respiratory Distress
1. Assess airway
2. 100% Oxygen
3. Position for comfort
4. Bronchodilators if wheezing
5. Prepare for intubation
6. Call EMS if not improving

### Malignant Hyperthermia
1. Stop triggers
2. Dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg
3. Hyperventilate with 100% O2
4. Cool patient
5. Treat hyperkalemia
6. Call MH Hotline

## Transfer to Hospital

### When to Transfer
| Indication | Action |
|------------|--------|
| Cardiac arrest | After stabilization or ongoing |
| Respiratory failure | If not quickly reversible |
| Major bleeding | If not controllable |
| Anaphylaxis | After initial treatment |
| Any life-threatening | After stabilization |

### Transfer Process
1. Stabilize patient
2. Call EMS
3. Notify receiving hospital
4. Prepare documentation
5. Accompany if possible
6. Sign out to receiving team

## Documentation

### Emergency Documentation
□ Time of event
□ What happened
□ Interventions performed
□ Medications given (dose, time)
□ Patient response
□ EMS involvement
□ Transfer details
□ Follow-up plan`,
